WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:07.370
- [Richard] Welcome
everybody to the public board

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:08.633
of NHS England.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.180
I should just start by noting

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.060
that I've taken what's
called a Chair's action,

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:15.680
which excludes members of the public

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.440
from physically being
within the same room as us.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:20.150
But clearly, this is being livestreamed,

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.340
and therefore the public
are able to witness

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.280
and observe the board and
the board's discussions.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.470
But we're hoping that perhaps
in the next board meeting,

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:28.790
actually we'll be able to do that

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:31.340
with the public in attendance.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.240
And we've had apologies for
absence from Jeremy Townsend,

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.150
who was with us earlier
today in the private board,

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:42.590
and also from Mike Coupe and Susan Kilsby.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:44.780
Can I ask if anyone has any interests

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.393
that they need to declare,
conflict of interest?

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:49.820
Thank you.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.120
And can I ask the board for its approval

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.590
for the minutes of the
public board from July?

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:58.493
Happy to approve?

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:01.040
Thank you. Welcome, Amanda.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.490
Let me just give a brief update as Chair.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:10.130
Just some reflections, if I might.

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:12.181
We're going to have a topic later on,

00:01:12.181 --> 00:01:16.490
a very distressing topic,
attaching to Edenfield,

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:19.310
where Claire Murdoch and colleagues will,

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:23.120
as part of Amanda's update,
give us their reflections.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.040
And clearly, it was a very distressing,

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.670
rather appalling picture
that we were presented with.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:33.350
I know that the board feels
a gut wrenching distress

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:34.910
at actually what we saw.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.970
But we will deal with that in a moment.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.070
I have actually, which is by coincidence,

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:42.770
done a number of visits recently,

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:46.280
including actually to a number
of mental health facilities.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.490
So, I went to the Newham,
Centre for Mental Health,

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:53.000
part of the East London
NHS Foundation Trust,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.420
and also did a session
meeting patients and parents,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:00.689
and these children and younger people,

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:03.623
who were struggling with
mental health issues.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.240
ELFT has been rated outstanding.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.853
And actually, I had a really good visit.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.030
I also actually, on Friday,

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:16.250
went to the Sussex Healthcare Partnership,

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:20.570
and visited a level three
mental health facility,

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:21.800
the Hellingley Centre.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.850
Again, was hugely impressed by the staff

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:28.165
and the commitment of the staff.

00:02:28.165 --> 00:02:30.830
And I went on from there
to the Eastbourne DGH,

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.203
to the mental health facility there.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.850
I would share, though, that
actually what is interesting,

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:39.890
and it resonates through a
number of our conversations,

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.140
is workforce pressures, actually,

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:43.640
and we see that across the health service,

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:47.690
but also in each of those areas,

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:50.840
clearly there's lots of
challenge on workforce hiring,

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.880
retention rates, and so on.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.970
I'd just like to do one
quick call out if I might.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.190
I had a recent visit to
Charing Cross Hospital

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:01.160
and actually saw a Maggie's centre.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:05.180
and Maggie's does wonderful
work in cancer support.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.380
24 of these centres.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.600
Started 25 years ago as a charity.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.140
Remarkable charity, and it
does brilliant work, actually.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:15.923
And I just wanted to give
a call out to Maggie's.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.270
If we look at the agenda,
we're going to have,

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.850
I think, a very good
discussion on primary care.

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:26.930
It remains a key priority
for us as the board.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.820
And we need also not just to look

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:29.780
at the immediate pressures,

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:34.670
but also to keep an eye on
the medium and longer term.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:37.040
And Amanda Doyle will be leading us,

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.600
I think, through that conversation.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.030
We are intending to
bring our full response

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:45.293
to the full review recommendations
in the December board.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.370
Of the system, it's
part of the system that,

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:53.030
in my own personal view,
seems under the most pressure.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.450
And yet actually, our colleagues
who work in primary care

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:00.800
do a hugely demanding job,
and they do it really well.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.500
And some of the things that
sometimes people lose sight of

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:06.080
is just the underlying volume of support

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.390
that's provided in primary
care to the population.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:13.390
We know that almost a million
appointments every single day

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.850
are offered in primary care in England.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:20.510
And of that million, 450,000
or so are appointments

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:23.090
on the same day as the
call actually was made

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:24.170
asking for an appointment.

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:26.270
So there's a huge volume of support,

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.030
but we also mustn't close
our eyes to the fact

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.277
that there is an access issue.

00:04:31.277 --> 00:04:32.570
And clearly, we need to be thinking

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:35.660
about how we can improve
support to our GPs

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:39.860
and our primary care workforce
as they look to service

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:41.993
and to serve the population.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:44.750
Interestingly, and we'll
see it some of the stats

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.220
and David confirmed it this morning,

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:48.770
if we look at the total
number of appointments,

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:51.470
we're running at more than 10% more

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:55.010
than we were running pre-pandemic
in number of appointments.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:56.720
Now, that has COVID vaccinations,

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:58.670
but even if you take the vaccinations out,

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:02.420
we're running at six to 7%
higher volume of appointments

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:04.520
being provided than actually primary care

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:08.603
was providing in in 2019.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.560
Dentistry, partly because
I had my teeth knocked out

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:13.400
by a cricket ball,

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:16.313
I have a particular interest in dentistry,

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.790
and we're clearly looking here

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:21.257
at both capacity and contractual issues.

00:05:21.257 --> 00:05:22.490
And in Amanda's section,

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:24.890
we're going to be talking about dentistry.

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:27.257
We haven't really looked at
it in detail for some time.

00:05:27.257 --> 00:05:29.603
And actually, one of the things, Amanda,

00:05:29.603 --> 00:05:32.360
we might do is have a
deep dive into dentistry,

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.963
perhaps at a future public board.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:39.380
I've talked about mental
health on the agenda,

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:43.220
and Claire will, in Amanda's CEO update,

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:45.980
give us views about actually
the actions we're taking

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:48.293
in response to what we saw.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:51.950
Ambulances in urgent and emergency care.

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:56.270
I read with some delight
in the Sunday newspapers,

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:59.930
actually, the experiment going
on London Ambulance Service,

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:02.540
piloting some fast response vehicles.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:06.020
Those fast response vehicles
essentially going out

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:08.300
either to engage in
mental health episodes,

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:09.620
or actually to deal with falls.

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:13.430
And what we do know in the
analysis in category two falls,

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:16.940
what we see clearly is
one of the main causes

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:18.920
of ambulance conveyance into hospitals

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:21.350
is elderly people falling,

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:23.240
and they're not being
responded to quickly enough.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.520
So, actually is there a
better way of dealing with it?

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:30.320
And I'm really pleased to see that pilot.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:32.450
I'd encourage it to be a bigger pilot

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:34.640
in all of the ambulance trusts frankly,

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:37.520
but I know the board know
that I can be quite fixated

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.453
on the fleet shape of
our ambulance service.

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:46.190
Cost of living. We're going
to get an update from Julian.

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:49.070
This is an issue. It's
not just an NHS issue.

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:50.660
It's an issue clearly
for the whole economy,

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:54.500
as we see inflation biting.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:56.377
I had a very good session with around 20

00:06:56.377 --> 00:06:59.330
of our HR directors about
two weeks or so ago,

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:00.650
sharing amongst themselves,

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:03.020
but sharing selfishly with me

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.030
a number of the initiatives
that they're undertaking

00:07:05.030 --> 00:07:06.800
to try and alleviate the pressure

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:09.530
of the cost of living
rises upon their staff.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:12.752
And I would commend that,
the work that was underway,

00:07:12.752 --> 00:07:16.010
not just the HR directors;
also the finance directors,

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:18.350
looking to find ways to support our staff

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:20.813
as we see these financial pressures.

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:24.050
Workforce plan.

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:26.960
We had a good discussion this
morning on the workforce plan.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.910
I think it's important that
we are ambitious in our aims

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:36.740
for the NHS, in terms of what
we imagine and can conceive

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:40.910
that workforce looking
like in three years' time,

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:43.070
in five years' time, in
nine, 10 years' time,

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.570
in 15 years' time.

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:47.090
And I would say the work
that's underway there,

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:49.850
led by Nabina, is excellent

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:52.910
and we're making very good progress.

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:55.640
We're also making good
progress on something I think

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:57.530
which perhaps people lose sight of,

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:01.220
which is the very complicated
programme of execution,

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:05.780
which is the merger, as we
know, of the five entities,

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:10.040
NHS England, Improvement,
Digital, X and HEE,

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:11.510
led by Mark Cubbon.

00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:13.100
I think that work is going really well.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.110
Wol is chairing that committee.

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:17.750
Again, we have a regular update on it,

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:22.070
and I think we can see
that we will be, to quote,

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:25.220
actually Tim Ferris, we will
be a much better organisation

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:26.480
once we're through it, actually,

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:29.960
in terms of I think simplicity,

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:31.580
engagement down through the systems.

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:33.260
And then for me the important principles

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:37.370
of de-bureaucratization,
decentralisation and trust.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:41.513
And that trust into the systems
I think is really important.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.360
Lastly, I think next
week I've got a meeting

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:49.670
with all of the ICB
chairs, or three meetings.

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:51.407
So we're splitting into three groups,

00:08:51.407 --> 00:08:52.880
and I'm looking forward to that.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:55.700
There clearly are a lot
of pressures the ICBs,

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.697
as they have come into emergence,
are having to deal with.

00:08:58.697 --> 00:09:01.130
And it's a very good
opportunity just to pick up

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:04.790
from our ICB chairs what
their reflections are,

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:06.980
and what their key issues are as we face,

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:11.810
really, I think what are
really quite challenging times.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:12.770
And then, just lastly was

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:14.930
we had a board dinner last night

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:16.700
and I was delighted, actually,

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.970
with all of our three guest speakers.

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:22.430
But Tim Briggs, I thought,
gave us a very good insight

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:24.260
actually into the work that can be done

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:27.320
around the elective backlog,
looking at refer to treat,

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:30.140
and thinking about how we can address

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:31.490
what is a growing backlog,

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:33.950
but where, as we know, it's 6.8 million,

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:36.860
but less than 3% actually requires surgery

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:38.180
requiring an overnight stay,

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:41.270
and it's around less than
20% or just under 20%

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:44.060
actually requires some surgical procedure,

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:45.500
therefore day surgery.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:47.960
So, there's 80% of that waiting list,

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:49.820
which is not actually requiring surgery,

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:51.680
so how do we manage that?

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:54.680
How do we cohort it and
how do we address that

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:58.340
to provide faster
support, prompter support

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.100
to the population, to patients?

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.621
That's all I would say
by way of introduction.

00:10:04.621 --> 00:10:08.548
I am happy to take any
questions or comments

00:10:08.548 --> 00:10:11.840
on anything I've said, if
anyone wants to, but if not,

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:13.130
I was going to hand over to Amanda

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:15.577
for chief executive's update.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:18.290
Okay, Amanda.

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:20.240
- [Amanda] Thank you. Thank you, Richard.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:22.850
So I've got a number of
things I wanted to cover

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:23.990
as part of my update,

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:26.780
but actually the first thing
I just wanted to acknowledge,

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:28.460
of course, is this is our
first meeting together

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:32.240
since the death of her late
Majesty Queen Elizabeth II,

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:35.060
who was a longtime supporter of the NHS,

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:37.370
particularly of our staff.

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:39.140
And just to note that, of course,

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:41.420
one of her last major public engagements

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:43.760
was to award not just the NHS in England

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:46.910
but the NHS in Scotland, Wales,
and Northern Ireland as well

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:48.260
with the George Cross.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:52.340
It was, I think, a huge
privilege to be part

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:56.090
of the state funeral arrangements

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:57.980
and in the procession afterwards,

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:00.710
to be represented once
again by May Parsons,

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:03.020
who of course was with me when
we received the George Cross,

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:06.650
as the first person to
give a COVID vaccination

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:08.510
outside a clinical trial in the world.

00:11:08.510 --> 00:11:10.880
So, just to thank May for that.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:13.430
But also, I think just to
acknowledge the incredible support

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:15.980
that we have had from Queen Elizabeth,

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:19.490
and continue to enjoy and really value

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:21.323
from the whole of the royal family.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:26.150
To build, Richard, if I may, on your words

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:30.710
about the images and
stories that were captured

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:32.240
in the Panorama programme,

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:34.310
which focused on the Edenfield Centre

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:36.290
in greater Manchester.

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:41.290
I'm very pleased to have
Claire, Tom, and Tim with us

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:44.600
to talk about this as part of my update.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:46.880
But I think I just want
to echo Richard's words,

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:48.860
and indeed I think the
words of the whole board,

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:50.690
when we were talking earlier

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:55.690
about just how personally
shocking and horrifying it was

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.943
for us, I think, watching some of that.

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:02.000
It is right, I think, that the conduct

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:05.480
of individuals involved is
appropriately investigated,

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:07.670
and we are working with
the Trust to ensure

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:10.130
that they cooperate fully
with these investigations,

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:13.070
as well of course as
taking immediate action

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:14.780
to safeguard current patients,

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:18.080
to fulfil their duties
towards previous patients

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:18.920
and their families,

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:22.010
and to prepare for the
independent investigations

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:25.130
which will come on the back of,

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:29.330
and pending the outcome of
current police investigations.

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:33.590
But I think, whilst we must
talk about that specific centre

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:36.110
and what happened there,
it is really important,

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:38.090
and this is where it's
great to have colleagues

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:39.710
to support this discussion,

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:41.300
that we don't allow ourselves to think

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:43.190
that this was a one off.

00:12:43.190 --> 00:12:46.460
So, it's why Claire wrote immediately out

00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:48.410
to chief executives of mental health

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:50.513
and learning disability service providers,

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:55.250
setting out a series of urgent
actions to assure themselves

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:57.530
and their boards that those key safety

00:12:57.530 --> 00:13:01.400
and experience measures are in
place around their services,

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:03.950
specifically including
safeguards and oversight

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:06.440
in relation to restrictive practises.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:09.680
But I think it's also
absolutely emphasised

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:14.330
the criticality of the
new quality programme,

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:16.610
which Claire and colleagues, I suspect,

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:19.160
will talk a bit about now.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:20.317
Is that right If we just turn to that?

00:13:20.317 --> 00:13:21.230
- [Richard] Please. I
think very important.

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:22.310
- Thank you.

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:23.303
- [Claire] Yes, thank you.

00:13:23.303 --> 00:13:24.950
And may I just introduce
the two colleagues

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:27.650
I've brought with me, or ask
them to introduce themselves?

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:29.480
- [Tim] Tim Kendall,
national clinical director

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:30.313
for mental health.

00:13:30.313 --> 00:13:32.900
I'm also a practising psychiatrist.

00:13:32.900 --> 00:13:34.940
- [Tom] Afternoon
everyone. I'm Tom Callum.

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:37.130
I'm national Director for
learning disability and autism,

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:40.040
and I used to be chief exec
of a Mental health Trust.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:41.090
- [Claire] Thank you.

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:44.360
So, I'll just take a
few minutes to set out

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:48.020
our initial response and
thoughts to these atrocities,

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:51.320
and they were what happened in Manchester

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:54.983
at the Edenfield Centre was
indefensible and appalling.

00:13:56.270 --> 00:14:00.710
We absolutely have asked the
sector to proceed on the basis,

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:02.840
and to review what
they're doing on the basis

00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:06.710
of it could happen here.

00:14:06.710 --> 00:14:11.450
We need boards of every
single provider and caregiver

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:16.310
to really review their existing
arrangements on that basis.

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:18.650
Is it happening here? How do we know?

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:21.950
Are our procedures, are
our processes enough?

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:22.880
Are they sufficient?

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:27.260
And in particular, are we working
with the public, patients,

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:30.080
people with lived experience
and their families,

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:32.390
to help us answer that question

00:14:32.390 --> 00:14:34.323
and help us think about how
we strengthen everything

00:14:34.323 --> 00:14:36.170
that we're doing?

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:39.140
We know this happens
elsewhere, we're sure of it.

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:41.060
We've had examples in the past.

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:43.850
We know of at least one
other example currently.

00:14:43.850 --> 00:14:46.310
So, we've asked for that to happen.

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:48.380
We also know there's
something about the nature

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:51.440
of these environments
where, let us not forget,

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:53.630
people don't choose to be there.

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:56.600
We detain them under sections
of the Mental Health Act.

00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:58.460
Often they lack capacity.

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:00.800
Always, they're extremely vulnerable.

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:05.180
And we intervene in their
lives in quite intimate aspects

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:09.920
of their care in a really critical way.

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:12.770
And therefore, care in
these units has to be better

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:13.970
than anywhere else.

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:18.970
But we know that they are
more susceptible to poor care,

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:24.220
and we have to take this
opportunity to double down

00:15:24.413 --> 00:15:27.320
on our safeguarding arrangements.

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:31.700
But it is the job of every board
in this country to do that.

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:33.650
And I would just like
to take the opportunity

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:37.190
as a registered nurse
myself to say is the role

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:39.650
of every registered professional,

00:15:39.650 --> 00:15:41.780
whatever their professional background,

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:44.540
to take this opportunity
to say, "What do I see?

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:45.710
What do I do?

00:15:45.710 --> 00:15:49.310
How can I play a role in
speaking up, changing care,

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:52.427
preventing care and tackling
this when it happens?"

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:56.000
And all of that must happen with people

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:57.323
with lived experience.

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:00.560
Coming to Greater Manchester, if I might,

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:02.123
in the Edenfield Centre,

00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:07.790
our Northwest region are
leading our response to that,

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:10.871
together with the ICB and the trust.

00:16:10.871 --> 00:16:14.777
They've moved immediately
to obviously put patients

00:16:14.777 --> 00:16:17.330
and their families at the
centre of the response.

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:21.230
They've taken immediate
steps to satisfy themselves

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:23.990
that existing patients are safe

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:26.930
and are the top priority
of what happens now.

00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:31.490
They've had the care of each
one of those people reviewed

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:33.590
by expert clinicians,

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:37.070
and now they're moving to
ask external clinicians

00:16:37.070 --> 00:16:39.950
from other trusts and
organisations to come in

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:41.720
and review their work.

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:44.840
They've also increased the
level of advocacy services,

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:49.280
i.e. non-clinical oversight
patient voice services

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:53.390
to be involved in that,
as well as families.

00:16:53.390 --> 00:16:58.160
They've suspended a
considerable number of staff

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:01.640
and they are proceeding
on the basis of evidence

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:06.640
in the Panorama programme to
set up several disciplinaries

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:09.320
or disciplinary processes for several

00:17:09.320 --> 00:17:14.320
of those staff immediately,
because we saw enough,

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:15.890
in some cases.

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:19.370
Their investigation into
other staff will go on

00:17:19.370 --> 00:17:20.510
for some time.

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:23.753
This is a long term, but
that is happening now.

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:28.280
In terms of, if I can
step back from Manchester

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:32.396
and this greater point of,
"It could happen here,"

00:17:32.396 --> 00:17:35.810
and what are we doing about
that and how do we approach it?

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:38.000
I want to talk very briefly about the work

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:42.350
that we've been doing on
changing the model of care.

00:17:42.350 --> 00:17:45.740
We absolutely have taken
the 2.3 billion pounds

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:48.920
worth of long-term plan investment

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:51.950
and put that into community services,

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:54.980
community alternatives
to both support people

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:56.510
to avoid crises,

00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:59.690
but also support them differently
when they go into crisis.

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:03.320
So, we know that the
overwhelming majority of patients

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:08.320
or people will do well in
community mental health services.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:11.210
In June of this year, for example,

00:18:11.210 --> 00:18:16.130
we saw 1.6 million patients
were on our caseload,

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:20.003
and nearly all of those patients
were in community settings.

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:23.480
We've increased staffing levels.

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:25.820
We've in fact increased
over the last three years,

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:29.420
staffing mainly in community
provision, for example,

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:31.070
by more than 20,000.

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:34.400
We were 114,000 staff working

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:37.400
in mental health settings in 2019.

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:42.260
We're currently 135,661 staff

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:44.840
working in mental health services.

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:48.080
So, we're doing a great
deal of work to expand

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:51.140
the community alternatives to care.

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:53.570
We want to go further
and look more radically

00:18:53.570 --> 00:18:54.530
at the model of care.

00:18:54.530 --> 00:18:58.310
And we are looking internationally
at who provides services

00:18:58.310 --> 00:19:00.440
in a different model of care.

00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:05.090
And examples would be, for
example, Trieste in Italy

00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:06.530
and elsewhere.

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:11.530
But we do know that, even in
that transformed model of care,

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:14.210
there will be people who do need to come

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:17.030
into hospital, for short periods, we hope,

00:19:17.030 --> 00:19:18.830
and when they come into hospital,

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:22.790
they have every right to
expect dignified, humane,

00:19:22.790 --> 00:19:25.403
evidence-based compassionate care.

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:29.360
So, we are going to make
further structural changes.

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:31.340
We've begun that.

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:35.660
But we do know that there are
some big structural issues

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:40.190
that make that challenging, the
chief of which is workforce.

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:44.480
Of course, we know for example
that despite the growth

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:46.460
in mental health care,

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:50.439
that we currently have between 14 and 28%

00:19:50.439 --> 00:19:54.347
qualified nurse vacancies in the country.

00:19:54.347 --> 00:19:56.390
That's by region.

00:19:56.390 --> 00:19:59.900
So I'm delighted that the chief
nurse and other colleagues

00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:03.500
are really working closely with
us on what we do about that.

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:04.907
But there are workforce pressures,

00:20:04.907 --> 00:20:06.770
and we must acknowledge those.

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:11.770
We must also acknowledge many
of the environmental issues;

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:16.130
the quality of the buildings,
access to fresh air,

00:20:16.130 --> 00:20:18.110
gardens, outside space.

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:22.520
So, we know that some units
are world class and wonderful,

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:24.620
but others simply are not good enough

00:20:24.620 --> 00:20:27.470
and we have to have a
relentless focus on that.

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:30.800
The final thing I'd like to say is that,

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:34.190
in view of the fact that
most of our efforts to date

00:20:34.190 --> 00:20:35.900
have been on changing the model of care

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:37.460
and will continue to be,

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:42.320
and expanding community
mental health facilities,

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:46.280
we do need to focus as well on the quality

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:47.600
of inpatient care.

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:50.840
Several weeks ago now, we
announced that we were going

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:55.490
to launch an inpatient
quality improvement programme.

00:20:55.490 --> 00:20:58.160
We've appointed a very
senior leader to lead that.

00:20:58.160 --> 00:20:59.930
They will do that with clinical leaders,

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:02.390
such as Professor Kendall and others,

00:21:02.390 --> 00:21:06.470
but also patients with lived
experience and their families.

00:21:06.470 --> 00:21:09.110
And we now have to scope that project.

00:21:09.110 --> 00:21:11.720
We're putting 36 million pounds

00:21:11.720 --> 00:21:14.150
against a three year
improvement programme.

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:16.343
That's 12 million pounds a year,

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:20.630
and it's more critical than ever.

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:23.718
- [Richard] Claire, can I-
- Work proceeds rapidly,

00:21:23.718 --> 00:21:25.160
and radically.

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:26.180
- [Richard] Thank you.

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:28.280
That last word, radically,
I think is important,

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:30.350
and we've had a number of conversations

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:32.480
with our members of the board.

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:35.180
You're quite right, I think,
that it's a governance

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:37.073
and performance problem.

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:43.280
It's an unacceptable behavioural problem.

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:45.440
It may go through to facilities

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:47.390
and other types of external pressures,

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:50.450
but what we have to remember
here is to keep the patient

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:54.023
absolutely at the forefront
of how we think about this.

00:21:55.070 --> 00:21:57.530
I'm pleased you've referenced the fact

00:21:57.530 --> 00:22:00.740
that we're going to look at
different models of care,

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:02.960
and I think that's a really
important piece of work

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:05.720
that gets done, and potentially
some quite radical options,

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:08.450
I think, in terms of insight and oversight

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:10.310
to the care that's delivered.

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:13.910
And I think that's also really important.

00:22:13.910 --> 00:22:16.400
I'm going to pause just
for a minute to see if any

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:17.720
colleagues just want to react.

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:20.480
Amanda, or any of my non-exec colleagues.

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:22.030
David, would you like to react?

00:22:23.270 --> 00:22:25.673
- [David] Claire, thank
you for your comments.

00:22:26.510 --> 00:22:28.040
I think, Richard, your position earlier,

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:30.710
of being on the side of
people that use services

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:32.720
is absolutely the right place to be.

00:22:32.720 --> 00:22:35.540
What happened was truly appalling,

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:38.840
and I think historically, this
isn't an isolated incident.

00:22:38.840 --> 00:22:42.110
I think the big challenge
is how do we extract

00:22:42.110 --> 00:22:44.360
the learning from this and
then translate that learning

00:22:44.360 --> 00:22:48.710
into our education and training
systems, our processes.

00:22:48.710 --> 00:22:51.500
A number of recently qualified nurses

00:22:51.500 --> 00:22:53.000
were featured on that programme,

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:55.670
which raises key questions
really about values,

00:22:55.670 --> 00:22:58.430
as well as behaviours,
and how are we training

00:22:58.430 --> 00:22:59.783
and educating people?

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:03.830
I thought the absence of other clinicians,

00:23:03.830 --> 00:23:06.710
on what we saw on the TV, that
doesn't mean they weren't,

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:08.150
but nevertheless is an issue.

00:23:08.150 --> 00:23:09.290
So I think, Richard,

00:23:09.290 --> 00:23:13.040
your point about what the
professional accountabilities are,

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:17.180
all registered professionals
make a personal commitment

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:20.360
to uphold the higher standards
of quality and safety.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:23.090
And I think they appear to have been

00:23:23.090 --> 00:23:26.993
quite seriously breached, by
the evidence of that programme.

00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:31.220
But I also think the governance issues;

00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:34.940
I think that trust has
been subject to incidents

00:23:34.940 --> 00:23:37.160
20 years ago on the Rowham ward.

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:39.200
And so, there is something

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:41.180
about what the organisational memory is,

00:23:41.180 --> 00:23:43.430
in spite of people changing,

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:45.770
that leads to the learning being extracted

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:48.350
and then being translated into practise.

00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:50.930
So, I think how we just
think about the governance

00:23:50.930 --> 00:23:52.790
going forward and whether that governance

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:53.960
has been effective.

00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:57.380
I think Claire, you're stressing
the board's responsibility,

00:23:57.380 --> 00:23:59.450
but also the senior
management responsibility

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:02.330
and the layers of management
I think is absolutely key.

00:24:02.330 --> 00:24:07.310
But I think the quality
improvement initiative,

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:11.000
and the investment of
both resources, physical,

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:13.640
human and financial, I think strikes me

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:16.340
as being an absolutely
appropriate thing to be doing.

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:19.043
I couldn't agree more
on the model of care,

00:24:20.952 --> 00:24:23.930
and just thinking differently about how,

00:24:23.930 --> 00:24:26.870
for the most vulnerable
people, we're offering care.

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:30.410
My fear is that perhaps,
we're warehousing people,

00:24:30.410 --> 00:24:32.180
rather than treating people with a view

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:33.260
to making them better.

00:24:33.260 --> 00:24:35.110
And how we make that shift, I think,

00:24:35.110 --> 00:24:36.500
is going to be one of the key challenges.

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:37.333
- [Richard] Thank you.

00:24:37.333 --> 00:24:38.166
Well, I know you want to make a comment

00:24:38.166 --> 00:24:41.870
and I might just hand to
Amanda to close this topic out.

00:24:41.870 --> 00:24:42.703
It's not to underestimate

00:24:42.703 --> 00:24:46.403
just how really, really
important this conversation is.

00:24:47.270 --> 00:24:50.960
But do you want to make a
comment or ask a question?

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:53.060
- [Wol] I agree fully
with David's comments

00:24:53.060 --> 00:24:56.000
and thank you for the
review you just gave us

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:58.670
about what's been happening
and what you're doing about it.

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:00.320
I want to make a very narrow point,

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:03.620
because we haven't discussed
much about culture.

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:06.860
We hinted at it. Underlying
all this is culture.

00:25:06.860 --> 00:25:08.600
And then there's also
a risk element to this.

00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:09.710
So, you again alluded to that;

00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:12.350
the fact that it could happen anywhere.

00:25:12.350 --> 00:25:15.080
So for me, it starts with
the governance dimension,

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:16.280
thinking, "If that's the case,

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:19.815
the first thing we talk about
is the risk of all that."

00:25:19.815 --> 00:25:22.970
And I'm wondering in your
further conversations

00:25:22.970 --> 00:25:24.650
with colleagues across the country,

00:25:24.650 --> 00:25:25.943
how much work you're going
to put into making sure

00:25:25.943 --> 00:25:28.703
that the culture is
adjusted to understand this,

00:25:28.703 --> 00:25:31.520
and to ensure that there's a
continual improvement process

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:33.890
going on so we don't
have this horrific stuff

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:35.540
happening again.

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:38.360
- [Claire] Yes, respond.
- [Richard] Please do, yeah.

00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:40.280
- [Claire] It may be actually
that one of my colleagues

00:25:40.280 --> 00:25:42.560
would like to, but
you're absolutely right.

00:25:42.560 --> 00:25:44.960
At the centre of this is culture, values,

00:25:44.960 --> 00:25:46.160
training and education.

00:25:46.160 --> 00:25:47.570
If the culture's not right,

00:25:47.570 --> 00:25:51.110
nothing else really can
reliably flow from that.

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:52.160
But I don't know.

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:55.910
- We have an existing
quality improvement programme

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:57.830
around restrictive interventions,

00:25:57.830 --> 00:26:00.020
and we've got every trust in the country

00:26:00.020 --> 00:26:01.640
already signed up to it.

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:05.420
But the early experience from
that is that if you change

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:09.560
the culture by having all
the improvement programmes

00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:13.910
planned by nurses,
doctors and service users,

00:26:13.910 --> 00:26:17.450
it changes the whole way in
which the culture operates,

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:21.050
and restrictive interventions
come down by 80%.

00:26:21.050 --> 00:26:24.320
So, I think you're absolutely
right. The culture is central.

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:25.760
- [Richard] And who engages on it.

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:27.830
Andrew, do you want to
make a comment then?

00:26:27.830 --> 00:26:28.670
- [Andrew] Just on that point,

00:26:28.670 --> 00:26:31.490
I think it's really important
that we work with the CQC

00:26:31.490 --> 00:26:36.490
going forward around how we
undertake the inspection process

00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:39.290
to surface these kind of things,

00:26:39.290 --> 00:26:41.270
because the health
service has had a history

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:44.270
of once every three or four
years, something like this,

00:26:44.270 --> 00:26:47.900
particularly in mental
health and LD services,

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:50.360
comes to fruition, unfortunately.

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:54.470
So, I think anything we
can do as regulators,

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:56.720
by working differently
together, we should.

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:59.630
And I would urge that the
work you're doing, Claire,

00:26:59.630 --> 00:27:01.933
is shared with colleagues in the CQC,

00:27:01.933 --> 00:27:05.630
because I think there'd be
lots of advantage downstream.

00:27:05.630 --> 00:27:09.140
The other thing is acute
providers and other providers

00:27:09.140 --> 00:27:10.760
need to learn from this as well.

00:27:10.760 --> 00:27:12.710
So, I've just noticed your note just went

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:16.070
to mental health chief execs, I think.

00:27:16.070 --> 00:27:17.000
- [Richard] Do you want to make one?

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:18.449
I can see you want make one.

00:27:18.449 --> 00:27:20.600
- The whole conversation about culture,

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:21.710
really, really important.

00:27:21.710 --> 00:27:24.890
The striking thing here that
this wasn't just poor care,

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:26.480
this wasn't just people getting it wrong,

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:29.600
and we obviously pick those
people up, we help them.

00:27:29.600 --> 00:27:32.270
There's malice. There's malice here.

00:27:32.270 --> 00:27:35.270
There's stuff that you wouldn't
expect to see on the street.

00:27:35.270 --> 00:27:38.300
So, it goes beyond the
culture, it goes to values.

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:39.380
It goes to recruitment.

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:42.880
It goes into that territory
which we do need to go into.

00:27:43.898 --> 00:27:45.560
- [Richard] Yeah, thank you.

00:27:45.560 --> 00:27:48.050
Amanda, do you want to
any last remarks on that,

00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:50.930
and then go into the
rest of your CEO report?

00:27:50.930 --> 00:27:52.977
But what we have agreed
is that I think we want

00:27:52.977 --> 00:27:56.420
to have a very close
direct board oversight of,

00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:58.580
I'll call it the remediation here.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:01.820
We're going to ask the quality committee

00:28:01.820 --> 00:28:03.710
to oversee that very directly,

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:05.930
but also bring it back
regularly to the board,

00:28:05.930 --> 00:28:08.960
just to see actually that
we do drive very firmly

00:28:08.960 --> 00:28:10.910
at a number of the points
that you've been making

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:11.743
to us today.

00:28:11.743 --> 00:28:13.580
But Amanda.
- I think you've just summed

00:28:13.580 --> 00:28:14.810
it up exactly, Richard.

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:17.060
Because I think there's an awful lot,

00:28:17.060 --> 00:28:18.830
even just in the
discussion we've just had,

00:28:18.830 --> 00:28:21.830
and making sure now that
we do follow through,

00:28:21.830 --> 00:28:25.640
specifically around the
circumstances in greater Manchester,

00:28:25.640 --> 00:28:28.100
on the broader reflections
that colleagues have shared

00:28:28.100 --> 00:28:31.070
about the importance of that
quality improvement programme

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:32.750
with culture at its heart.

00:28:32.750 --> 00:28:35.870
But also, that we take the
opportunity to continue that work

00:28:35.870 --> 00:28:38.390
on the model of care and exactly the way

00:28:38.390 --> 00:28:39.890
that David and Claire talked about.

00:28:39.890 --> 00:28:42.290
So, I think though across all
of those three dimensions,

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:44.567
Richard, there's a lot of work to do,

00:28:44.567 --> 00:28:47.630
and just grateful to
colleagues for coming today,

00:28:47.630 --> 00:28:49.550
but also for all of the
work they have already done

00:28:49.550 --> 00:28:52.220
and will do as we support
the whole of the NHS

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:55.550
to learn from this in exactly
the way you've described.

00:28:55.550 --> 00:28:58.130
So, if I just move on from that then,

00:28:58.130 --> 00:29:00.706
a few other things I wanted to cover.

00:29:00.706 --> 00:29:02.690
Firstly, of course, also worth noting

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:05.570
that since we last met, we
have a new secretary of state

00:29:05.570 --> 00:29:07.460
and ministerial team.

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:11.360
We've begun working with them
in these first few weeks.

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:13.340
And in particular, it's
been, I think, very helpful

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:16.550
to get early clarity on the priorities

00:29:16.550 --> 00:29:20.180
for the current secretary
of state set out in our plan

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:24.320
for patients, which that's
the title of it, not our plan,

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:27.710
the plan for patients, titled,
"Our plan for patients,"

00:29:27.710 --> 00:29:30.350
which reflected priorities which I think

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:32.630
would absolutely recognise and share

00:29:32.630 --> 00:29:34.610
around urgent emergency care,

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:37.070
around the elective
backlogs, around social care,

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:39.410
which we've talked about at
the board many times before.

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:41.540
And of course, primary
care in its widest sense

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:46.430
of absolutely explicitly
covering dentistry, as well.

00:29:46.430 --> 00:29:48.380
So, I'd really like to
thank again everyone

00:29:48.380 --> 00:29:50.030
who's been involved in
working with colleagues

00:29:50.030 --> 00:29:51.530
to shape the plan.

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:55.250
And now, of course, we move
very firmly into continuing

00:29:55.250 --> 00:29:57.590
our focus on delivery.

00:29:57.590 --> 00:29:59.780
So, just talking about delivery:

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:01.640
the first thing I think
I'd just like to do

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:06.640
is to credit the work that
the vaccine team have done.

00:30:07.880 --> 00:30:10.610
Even though I know that
there isn't, perhaps,

00:30:10.610 --> 00:30:14.810
the same focus now as there was
last year in the public mind

00:30:14.810 --> 00:30:17.930
around COVID, actually it
is worth us noting that,

00:30:17.930 --> 00:30:20.780
as of today we have over nine
and a half thousand people

00:30:20.780 --> 00:30:22.640
in hospital with COVID.

00:30:22.640 --> 00:30:24.890
So, that is quite a significant increase

00:30:24.890 --> 00:30:27.110
just in the last few days.

00:30:27.110 --> 00:30:30.890
And therefore, the importance
of the vaccine programme

00:30:30.890 --> 00:30:34.460
in helping people to do what they can

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:39.460
to stay well over this winter
period is just as important

00:30:40.340 --> 00:30:42.380
as it ever has been.

00:30:42.380 --> 00:30:46.288
So, great start to the
programme with 5 million

00:30:46.288 --> 00:30:49.280
booster doses already
given, but of course,

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:51.680
a long way to go and some
of the same challenges

00:30:51.680 --> 00:30:55.970
about making sure that
it is really a service

00:30:55.970 --> 00:30:58.460
that really does reach out

00:30:58.460 --> 00:31:01.310
to traditionally underserved communities,

00:31:01.310 --> 00:31:03.050
and builds on all of the great learning

00:31:03.050 --> 00:31:06.800
from the last year of
the vaccine programme.

00:31:06.800 --> 00:31:10.100
So, thinking about COVID
pressures taking us

00:31:10.100 --> 00:31:13.523
into then a bit of a
focus, if I may, on winter.

00:31:15.410 --> 00:31:18.737
We have been working with
colleagues across the NHS

00:31:18.737 --> 00:31:21.890
for some time to make
sure that we were able

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:26.890
to be as well prepared
as possible for winter.

00:31:27.410 --> 00:31:30.830
So, we wrote out something
some six weeks plus ago

00:31:30.830 --> 00:31:33.290
with a range of actions
that we were asking

00:31:33.290 --> 00:31:34.520
colleagues to take.

00:31:34.520 --> 00:31:37.970
We are meeting again with
the NHS leadership community

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:42.410
next week to talk about
what more we can do

00:31:42.410 --> 00:31:44.090
over above those things.

00:31:44.090 --> 00:31:48.410
Recognising that, if we
think about the risks

00:31:48.410 --> 00:31:50.840
around COVID, the risks around flu,

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:54.530
the risks around some of the
cost of living, which is still,

00:31:54.530 --> 00:31:56.330
I know, uppermost in people's minds,

00:31:56.330 --> 00:31:58.190
you mentioned it a moment ago,

00:31:58.190 --> 00:32:00.140
and of course the additional risk

00:32:00.140 --> 00:32:02.480
of potential industrial
action over winter.

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:05.840
We are looking at a really, I think,

00:32:05.840 --> 00:32:10.760
potentially quite
significant set of challenges

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:12.710
over this winter period.

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:15.950
We have gone early, gone in August,

00:32:15.950 --> 00:32:17.900
with a set of actions
that we're already seeing

00:32:17.900 --> 00:32:18.733
making a difference.

00:32:18.733 --> 00:32:20.940
If we just look at the number
of beds we have open now,

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:25.190
it is significantly more than
we had this time last year.

00:32:25.190 --> 00:32:27.110
So, we can see the work
that colleagues have done

00:32:27.110 --> 00:32:29.750
across the NHS to increase capacity.

00:32:29.750 --> 00:32:31.730
But as I say, we have
been challenging ourselves

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:33.530
and working with colleagues on what more,

00:32:33.530 --> 00:32:36.590
and we have our conversation
with leaders next week

00:32:36.590 --> 00:32:38.210
to explore that in more detail.

00:32:38.210 --> 00:32:40.673
Couple of things to say on the what more:

00:32:41.630 --> 00:32:45.710
primary care access is a
hugely important strand

00:32:45.710 --> 00:32:47.240
of what more,

00:32:47.240 --> 00:32:50.480
and I just wanted to note
how encouraging it was

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:52.610
to see the story in the Mail, actually,

00:32:52.610 --> 00:32:54.950
on recruitment of advanced practitioners,

00:32:54.950 --> 00:32:59.180
GP assistants and digital
leads to join the thousands

00:32:59.180 --> 00:33:02.690
of additional roles that help
people get the care they need

00:33:02.690 --> 00:33:05.810
and help support, as you
said a moment ago, Richard,

00:33:05.810 --> 00:33:10.520
our colleagues in primary
care who do so much

00:33:10.520 --> 00:33:13.640
every single day, in response
to the needs of the public.

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:15.980
But we absolutely know
they need more support,

00:33:15.980 --> 00:33:19.070
and it was great to see
those new roles recognised

00:33:19.070 --> 00:33:22.400
as part of what we can do to help.

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:24.380
The other thing that I wanted to call out,

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:25.790
particularly in relation to primary care,

00:33:25.790 --> 00:33:27.620
which is also really good to see,

00:33:27.620 --> 00:33:30.830
was the news that community
pharmacy consultation service

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:35.830
has already ramped up to help
over 118,000 people in June.

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:40.220
That's double the number
from June last year.

00:33:40.220 --> 00:33:43.730
So, I think we are aware that
community pharmacy provide

00:33:43.730 --> 00:33:45.350
an invaluable role anyway,

00:33:45.350 --> 00:33:48.110
but also they're at the
heart of local communities,

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:50.590
providing services that
would otherwise take people

00:33:50.590 --> 00:33:55.590
to GP services or potentially
even into treatment centres

00:33:56.180 --> 00:33:57.470
or into A and E.

00:33:57.470 --> 00:34:01.070
So, absolutely great to see that service

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:04.850
really beginning to take off a real scale.

00:34:04.850 --> 00:34:06.050
Final thing I also wanted to say:

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:07.130
when we think about capacity,

00:34:07.130 --> 00:34:08.570
we think about what we can do for winter.

00:34:08.570 --> 00:34:10.940
Of course, I know we've
done this as a board before,

00:34:10.940 --> 00:34:13.460
but I think hugely important
that we acknowledge

00:34:13.460 --> 00:34:18.020
the role that volunteers can
play, and continue to play,

00:34:18.020 --> 00:34:22.760
to support the NHS and improve
the experience of patients

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:26.240
and of staff will continue
to be really important

00:34:26.240 --> 00:34:28.850
volunteering over this coming winter.

00:34:28.850 --> 00:34:32.240
And we are asking all ICBs to think now

00:34:32.240 --> 00:34:34.820
about how they can develop the
volunteering infrastructure

00:34:34.820 --> 00:34:35.960
within their systems,

00:34:35.960 --> 00:34:39.800
recognising that value that the
local volunteering workforce

00:34:39.800 --> 00:34:43.670
and local volunteering support
and initiatives can provide.

00:34:43.670 --> 00:34:45.080
We're supporting ICBs to do this.

00:34:45.080 --> 00:34:47.900
Ruth has been leading this
work in a variety of ways,

00:34:47.900 --> 00:34:51.200
including remodelling the NHS
volunteer responders programme

00:34:51.200 --> 00:34:54.320
to better integrate with
local health systems

00:34:54.320 --> 00:34:55.820
and voluntary organisations,

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:58.670
to continue to provide a
national crisis response,

00:34:58.670 --> 00:35:01.580
supporting ICBs to set up local alliances

00:35:01.580 --> 00:35:02.960
with voluntary community

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:05.030
and social enterprise organisations.

00:35:05.030 --> 00:35:08.240
I gather we now have 42
of those live already,

00:35:08.240 --> 00:35:10.010
and we've also been doing things

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:11.240
that I think you're already aware of.

00:35:11.240 --> 00:35:15.170
So commissioning surge,
discharge support for trusts,

00:35:15.170 --> 00:35:18.950
and commissioning St. John to
provide additional auxiliary

00:35:18.950 --> 00:35:21.770
ambulance support to
areas of highest need.

00:35:21.770 --> 00:35:25.010
So we're hoping, I think,
to do more on volunteering

00:35:25.010 --> 00:35:26.210
over the course of the next year,

00:35:26.210 --> 00:35:27.950
but just to acknowledge that that remains

00:35:27.950 --> 00:35:29.600
a really important part of our resilience

00:35:29.600 --> 00:35:32.030
and our response over
winter, and to thank again,

00:35:32.030 --> 00:35:34.880
all of those who give their
time to support the NHS,

00:35:34.880 --> 00:35:38.000
to support our patients
and to support our staff.

00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:40.130
Richard, I wasn't going to say more

00:35:40.130 --> 00:35:41.720
about the financial position,

00:35:41.720 --> 00:35:44.630
because we will cover that in a moment,

00:35:44.630 --> 00:35:46.280
in the positional planning.

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:47.240
But I think just worth noting

00:35:47.240 --> 00:35:49.490
that is an important
conversation for today.

00:35:49.490 --> 00:35:51.530
And then just finally,
to acknowledge the point

00:35:51.530 --> 00:35:53.420
that you made a moment go,

00:35:53.420 --> 00:35:56.900
which is just to say, I
suppose to my colleagues

00:35:56.900 --> 00:36:00.710
within this organisation,
as much as to the board,

00:36:00.710 --> 00:36:04.520
that we are aware that it is a
hugely complex piece of work,

00:36:04.520 --> 00:36:07.940
the merger, and bringing
together of the five entities,

00:36:07.940 --> 00:36:09.173
as you described it.

00:36:12.899 --> 00:36:16.700
It is a very ambitious timeframe,
and there is a lot of work

00:36:16.700 --> 00:36:19.340
going on, led brilliantly by Mark,

00:36:19.340 --> 00:36:23.690
but really with now increasing
amount of engagement and time

00:36:23.690 --> 00:36:25.220
from colleagues across the organisation

00:36:25.220 --> 00:36:26.990
to do the design work,

00:36:26.990 --> 00:36:28.293
and make sure that we are in a position

00:36:28.293 --> 00:36:31.130
to be able to stick to the
time scales that we've given

00:36:31.130 --> 00:36:34.280
for what is a very big change programme.

00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:37.610
Very positively though, we
heard about this earlier,

00:36:37.610 --> 00:36:39.350
that we are now really beginning to see

00:36:39.350 --> 00:36:42.710
how we can design in simplification,

00:36:42.710 --> 00:36:46.130
and design in better ways of working,

00:36:46.130 --> 00:36:48.920
that will give us that better organisation

00:36:48.920 --> 00:36:50.000
at the end of this.

00:36:50.000 --> 00:36:52.460
But I am just conscious
that it is nonetheless

00:36:52.460 --> 00:36:57.230
an unsettling time for staff
and colleagues want clarity

00:36:57.230 --> 00:36:59.330
and certainty, and
certainly our commitment

00:36:59.330 --> 00:37:01.910
is to keep briefing, keep communicating,

00:37:01.910 --> 00:37:05.090
and as soon as we possibly
can to give people

00:37:05.090 --> 00:37:08.570
the information that they are looking for

00:37:08.570 --> 00:37:10.712
about their own roles
and their own personal-

00:37:10.712 --> 00:37:15.080
- If I might just add
also, thanks to Laura,

00:37:15.080 --> 00:37:17.780
chairing digital, and
all the board of digital,

00:37:17.780 --> 00:37:20.030
and David chairing HEE
and the board of HEE,

00:37:20.030 --> 00:37:22.910
because I think the boards
are working well together,

00:37:22.910 --> 00:37:27.470
in what is a really challenging
execution programme.

00:37:27.470 --> 00:37:30.713
Thank you and your boards
for your engagement.

00:37:32.090 --> 00:37:34.190
- That's it for me.
- Very good.

00:37:34.190 --> 00:37:38.480
Any comments or questions for
Amanda on anything she said?

00:37:38.480 --> 00:37:39.593
Any reflections?

00:37:41.016 --> 00:37:42.680
Okay.

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:43.880
David, I think it's to you, isn't it?

00:37:43.880 --> 00:37:47.150
- [David] Yes. Thank you.
- The operational performance

00:37:47.150 --> 00:37:49.250
update and then we're
going to bring Julian in

00:37:49.250 --> 00:37:50.690
on financial performance.

00:37:50.690 --> 00:37:51.770
- [David] Okay, thank you Richard.

00:37:51.770 --> 00:37:53.630
You have in your papers, as ever,

00:37:53.630 --> 00:37:57.170
the comprehensive operations
report that covers all areas

00:37:57.170 --> 00:38:00.050
of activity, which I know
people read assiduously.

00:38:00.050 --> 00:38:01.670
As such, I won't go through every word,

00:38:01.670 --> 00:38:03.830
nor will I page turn, but
I will with colleagues

00:38:03.830 --> 00:38:07.160
touch on four areas; namely
where we are with COVID,

00:38:07.160 --> 00:38:10.460
a bit more granularity on
the vaccination campaign.

00:38:10.460 --> 00:38:12.110
Then I will touch on elective recovery,

00:38:12.110 --> 00:38:13.760
and where we are with urgent care.

00:38:13.760 --> 00:38:14.960
So I will bring colleagues in.

00:38:14.960 --> 00:38:17.210
So Steve, could you share
with the board where we are

00:38:17.210 --> 00:38:19.310
with the COVID pandemic?

00:38:19.310 --> 00:38:20.143
- [Steve] Yeah, thank you David.

00:38:20.143 --> 00:38:22.490
And as you've already heard from Amanda,

00:38:22.490 --> 00:38:26.420
we have now entered the
fourth wave of COVID this year

00:38:26.420 --> 00:38:30.230
to impact upon hospitals
and health systems,

00:38:30.230 --> 00:38:33.470
with, as you heard, around nine
and a half thousand patients

00:38:33.470 --> 00:38:36.380
in our hospitals with confirmed COVID,

00:38:36.380 --> 00:38:39.770
and that is heading over 10,000.

00:38:39.770 --> 00:38:41.120
What's driving it?

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:44.690
Well, colleagues in public health tell us

00:38:44.690 --> 00:38:48.410
that Omicron, in particular
BA five and its sub-lineages,

00:38:48.410 --> 00:38:50.810
remain the dominant variant in England,

00:38:50.810 --> 00:38:53.600
although there are a number of
other sub-variants in the mix

00:38:53.600 --> 00:38:55.430
they are keeping a close eye on.

00:38:55.430 --> 00:38:58.130
So, the difference is
that, unlike the July wave,

00:38:58.130 --> 00:39:00.950
which was driven predominantly by BA five

00:39:00.950 --> 00:39:03.140
becoming the dominant variant,

00:39:03.140 --> 00:39:06.440
there is no new dominant
variant occurring in this wave.

00:39:06.440 --> 00:39:08.600
So, that means it's likely
that it's a combination

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:12.050
of waning immunity, and then reinfection,

00:39:12.050 --> 00:39:16.040
and that some of these variants
have mutations within them

00:39:16.040 --> 00:39:19.220
that avoid the immune
system or the immunity

00:39:19.220 --> 00:39:22.430
that's been developed
to previous infections

00:39:22.430 --> 00:39:23.300
or to vaccination.

00:39:23.300 --> 00:39:25.160
Not to say that vaccination
isn't successful,

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:27.740
as I'll come to in a minute.

00:39:27.740 --> 00:39:31.310
So all of that means
we're into another wave.

00:39:31.310 --> 00:39:34.760
The more positive news is that
the percentage of patients

00:39:34.760 --> 00:39:37.340
in high dependence units
and intensive care units

00:39:37.340 --> 00:39:40.130
is still at around two to 3% of the total.

00:39:40.130 --> 00:39:43.010
So, that's very similar to what we've seen

00:39:43.010 --> 00:39:46.370
since Omicron became the dominant
strain earlier this year,

00:39:46.370 --> 00:39:48.950
and very different from
the position last year,

00:39:48.950 --> 00:39:52.880
when it was typically around
17% of patients were in ITU.

00:39:52.880 --> 00:39:56.600
So, severity is less, and
a large part of that is due

00:39:56.600 --> 00:39:59.663
to the success of the vaccine programme.

00:40:00.560 --> 00:40:03.860
Again, the number of patients
or the percentage of patients

00:40:03.860 --> 00:40:07.580
where COVID is the primary
diagnosis in hospital

00:40:07.580 --> 00:40:10.100
is around the 35 to 40% mark,

00:40:10.100 --> 00:40:12.530
which again is similar to what we've seen

00:40:12.530 --> 00:40:16.280
since Omicron became dominant in January.

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:20.000
So, no change in those
particular statistics.

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:22.760
Now, it's important to say
that the ONS, of course,

00:40:22.760 --> 00:40:26.540
publishes data every week on
their COVID infection survey.

00:40:26.540 --> 00:40:28.490
They last published last Friday.

00:40:28.490 --> 00:40:30.200
But that was looking at data,

00:40:30.200 --> 00:40:32.210
up to the end of the week
of the 17th of September,

00:40:32.210 --> 00:40:34.250
which is already three weeks ago.

00:40:34.250 --> 00:40:35.720
That will be brought up to date, I'm sure,

00:40:35.720 --> 00:40:37.970
tomorrow with their next
publication brought up to date

00:40:37.970 --> 00:40:39.200
by another week.

00:40:39.200 --> 00:40:42.140
But I think what it means is
that the data we are seeing

00:40:42.140 --> 00:40:45.200
reported from hospitals is
now a bit ahead of the data

00:40:45.200 --> 00:40:47.570
that is being reported by the ONS.

00:40:47.570 --> 00:40:51.350
So, I think that's really
important to keep in mind.

00:40:51.350 --> 00:40:54.200
Where do we think that this will go?

00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:57.140
Well, I think it's certainly
possible that we will,

00:40:57.140 --> 00:40:59.330
over the next few weeks,
see a peak in this wave,

00:40:59.330 --> 00:41:02.000
which is similar to the
peaks that we have seen

00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:05.840
in the other three waves this year.

00:41:05.840 --> 00:41:08.840
And of course, that is
putting pressure on hospitals

00:41:08.840 --> 00:41:09.673
and health systems.

00:41:09.673 --> 00:41:11.630
So, I'm sure we'll hear in a minute.

00:41:11.630 --> 00:41:13.970
We have got infection prevention
control measures in place,

00:41:13.970 --> 00:41:16.940
and we have given trusts a
discretion to flex those,

00:41:16.940 --> 00:41:19.850
according to the particular circumstances

00:41:19.850 --> 00:41:21.140
they find themselves in.

00:41:21.140 --> 00:41:22.690
But of course, what's most important is

00:41:22.690 --> 00:41:23.798
the vaccine programme,

00:41:23.798 --> 00:41:27.380
because the main defence
we have against COVID

00:41:27.380 --> 00:41:30.170
is the vaccine programme
which is successful

00:41:30.170 --> 00:41:34.250
at both reducing transmission
over a period of time,

00:41:34.250 --> 00:41:37.100
but even more importantly,
at keeping that pressure

00:41:37.100 --> 00:41:39.170
on the prevention of severe disease.

00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:40.970
Because of course it is the severe disease

00:41:40.970 --> 00:41:45.830
that puts most pressure on us
in terms of hospitalisation.

00:41:45.830 --> 00:41:48.200
So, I think that would be a
good point to remind everybody,

00:41:48.200 --> 00:41:50.840
that when you get your notification

00:41:50.840 --> 00:41:52.820
that you're eligible for your booster,

00:41:52.820 --> 00:41:55.730
you should book it immediately
to protect yourself

00:41:55.730 --> 00:41:56.900
and your loved ones,

00:41:56.900 --> 00:41:59.720
and it's a good opportunity
to move to Steve to update us

00:41:59.720 --> 00:42:00.800
on the vaccine programme.

00:42:00.800 --> 00:42:01.712
- [David] Thank you Steve.

00:42:01.712 --> 00:42:03.170
I've booked in for mine next Saturday.

00:42:03.170 --> 00:42:04.490
- [Steve] Excellent.

00:42:04.490 --> 00:42:06.110
- [David] So, Sir Steve.

00:42:06.110 --> 00:42:07.970
- [Steve] Thank you,
David. Thank you, Steve.

00:42:07.970 --> 00:42:11.600
So, I'm delighted that the
NHS vaccination programme

00:42:11.600 --> 00:42:13.340
has got off to a good start.

00:42:13.340 --> 00:42:16.550
We're four weeks in and that is due

00:42:16.550 --> 00:42:19.760
to the very, very hard work
of NHS staff up and down

00:42:19.760 --> 00:42:24.110
the country, supported by a
large number of volunteers.

00:42:24.110 --> 00:42:28.580
As Amanda has said, last
week there was 22,000 hours

00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:31.880
of NHS volunteers into
the vaccination programme,

00:42:31.880 --> 00:42:34.553
and we see that increasing
every single week.

00:42:35.780 --> 00:42:40.780
As Steve has said, the combined
effect of flu and COVID

00:42:40.850 --> 00:42:42.500
means that the vaccination programme

00:42:42.500 --> 00:42:46.010
is a really important tool in protecting

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:49.820
against serious illness, hospitalisation,

00:42:49.820 --> 00:42:52.607
and also reducing pressure on the NHS.

00:42:52.607 --> 00:42:56.480
And I guess the NHS's job
is to make it as convenient

00:42:56.480 --> 00:43:00.590
as possible to all communities
to access a vaccination

00:43:00.590 --> 00:43:01.423
this autumn.

00:43:03.260 --> 00:43:04.970
As I said, we're about four weeks in.

00:43:04.970 --> 00:43:08.840
As Amanda said, just over 5 million people

00:43:08.840 --> 00:43:12.530
in eligible groups have come
forward for a vaccination

00:43:12.530 --> 00:43:16.250
across 3000 sites in England.

00:43:16.250 --> 00:43:19.100
And again, just to build on
the point colleagues have made,

00:43:19.100 --> 00:43:22.550
that's across general
practise, community pharmacy,

00:43:22.550 --> 00:43:26.390
larger scale vaccination
sites and really importantly,

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:29.480
lots of outreach activities
so that it is delivered

00:43:29.480 --> 00:43:33.173
in a way that is meaningful
to diverse communities.

00:43:34.370 --> 00:43:37.670
We've continued to focus on
those who are most at risk

00:43:37.670 --> 00:43:39.590
from COVID and flu.

00:43:39.590 --> 00:43:40.940
Excuse me.

00:43:40.940 --> 00:43:44.000
And over seven and a
half thousand care homes

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:44.960
have now had a visit,

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:48.500
which is about half of
the care homes in England.

00:43:48.500 --> 00:43:53.500
And two in five people who are
over 80 have already received

00:43:53.690 --> 00:43:55.790
their top-up protection.

00:43:55.790 --> 00:43:58.520
As Steve said though, it's
absolutely really important

00:43:58.520 --> 00:44:01.280
that people book into
one of the many sites

00:44:01.280 --> 00:44:03.743
that are available when they're invited.

00:44:04.760 --> 00:44:07.070
Alongside COVID, the NHS has delivered

00:44:07.070 --> 00:44:10.070
about four million flu
vaccinations, and again,

00:44:10.070 --> 00:44:13.520
I think we would absolutely
emphasise the importance

00:44:13.520 --> 00:44:16.310
of a flu vaccine this autumn.

00:44:16.310 --> 00:44:19.310
In many cases it's possible
to get those together.

00:44:19.310 --> 00:44:20.840
Some people will choose
to have them separately,

00:44:20.840 --> 00:44:22.840
and that is, of course, absolutely fine.

00:44:24.290 --> 00:44:26.900
So, I think, just to
close it, I guess, David,

00:44:26.900 --> 00:44:28.310
as colleagues have said,

00:44:28.310 --> 00:44:30.310
whilst we're off to a really good start,

00:44:31.400 --> 00:44:35.270
we've got to make sure that
underserved communities

00:44:35.270 --> 00:44:40.270
are well, well better served
by the NHS in this campaign.

00:44:40.310 --> 00:44:43.940
I think what we see in
all of the regional teams

00:44:43.940 --> 00:44:46.580
is people working
incredibly hard to make sure

00:44:46.580 --> 00:44:50.570
that the NHS works as
hard as it possibly can

00:44:50.570 --> 00:44:53.480
to ensure that we protect
as many people as possible.

00:44:53.480 --> 00:44:56.870
We know that it takes some
people longer to come forward,

00:44:56.870 --> 00:44:59.090
and we want to make sure that they're able

00:44:59.090 --> 00:45:01.730
to access a vaccination in
a way that is meaningful

00:45:01.730 --> 00:45:03.170
for them.

00:45:03.170 --> 00:45:06.680
And so, I think the risks,
just to highlight to the board,

00:45:06.680 --> 00:45:10.250
that we continue to work on,
is trying to set vaccination

00:45:10.250 --> 00:45:12.173
in the context of people's lives.

00:45:13.160 --> 00:45:15.820
People don't always start where
we think they might start,

00:45:15.820 --> 00:45:17.871
or where we want them to start.

00:45:17.871 --> 00:45:19.520
People have got really busy lives,

00:45:19.520 --> 00:45:22.070
with many other pressures on them.

00:45:22.070 --> 00:45:25.490
And so, our job is to try and build trust

00:45:25.490 --> 00:45:27.260
with local communities,

00:45:27.260 --> 00:45:29.600
but also, as Steve's very eloquently done,

00:45:29.600 --> 00:45:33.800
highlight the risks that still
persist around COVID and flu

00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:36.650
to reduce complacency,
because as Amanda said

00:45:36.650 --> 00:45:39.110
in her opening, it's not
top of everybody's mind,

00:45:39.110 --> 00:45:42.080
but still is a very
clear and present threat.

00:45:42.080 --> 00:45:44.750
So, continuing to work
on building confidence

00:45:44.750 --> 00:45:46.673
through local trusted voices,

00:45:48.230 --> 00:45:50.810
and reducing complacency in communities

00:45:50.810 --> 00:45:54.914
is the key task for the
rest of the programme.

00:45:54.914 --> 00:45:56.056
- [Richard] Okay, thank you Steve.

00:45:56.056 --> 00:46:00.230
- [Wol] Sorry Steve, you
highlighted or mentioned

00:46:00.230 --> 00:46:02.716
a few times, hard to reach communities.

00:46:02.716 --> 00:46:04.880
Could you just unpack a
little bit of that for us?

00:46:04.880 --> 00:46:08.660
Because as I understand it,
the number's not encouraging,

00:46:08.660 --> 00:46:11.480
both in terms of the
uptake by certain groups,

00:46:11.480 --> 00:46:14.663
but also the funding that's
made available by DHSC.

00:46:15.590 --> 00:46:17.030
- Thanks, Wol.

00:46:17.030 --> 00:46:18.710
What we've seen, I think
it's probably the better way

00:46:18.710 --> 00:46:22.310
to frame it is communities
that have not had

00:46:22.310 --> 00:46:24.800
as much confidence in public institutions,

00:46:24.800 --> 00:46:27.833
and have not necessarily
been as well served by us.

00:46:29.270 --> 00:46:33.350
And we have seen differentials
in all of the previous phases

00:46:33.350 --> 00:46:34.910
of the COVID campaign,

00:46:34.910 --> 00:46:38.120
which is reflected in other
vaccination programmes

00:46:38.120 --> 00:46:42.890
between communities from
different ethnic backgrounds,

00:46:42.890 --> 00:46:45.920
but also very importantly deprivation.

00:46:45.920 --> 00:46:48.170
And of course, those two things intersect.

00:46:48.170 --> 00:46:49.870
- [Wol] Yeah.
- Very importantly.

00:46:51.650 --> 00:46:55.250
So, we're in four weeks in,

00:46:55.250 --> 00:46:59.330
but we still see a difference
in uptake for people

00:46:59.330 --> 00:47:04.280
who are white British, compared
to people, for example,

00:47:04.280 --> 00:47:07.820
from a Pakistani and
Bangladeshi community.

00:47:07.820 --> 00:47:10.490
So, if you say the uptake in
the white British community

00:47:10.490 --> 00:47:15.350
was 100%, it's currently
about 62% for people

00:47:15.350 --> 00:47:17.453
from a Pakistan and Bangladeshi community.

00:47:18.440 --> 00:47:21.620
We've done quite a lot of
insight work with communities

00:47:21.620 --> 00:47:24.353
to try and understand how
we best tailor the offer.

00:47:25.220 --> 00:47:28.850
And that is all about local leadership.

00:47:28.850 --> 00:47:30.920
Local community presence.

00:47:30.920 --> 00:47:33.260
So, the vaccination centres are great.

00:47:33.260 --> 00:47:35.000
Community pharmacy is really important

00:47:35.000 --> 00:47:36.140
in communities like this.

00:47:36.140 --> 00:47:39.630
Community pharmacy is one
of the only settings of care

00:47:40.520 --> 00:47:42.473
that beats the inverse care law.

00:47:43.640 --> 00:47:46.970
And so, continuing to do local,

00:47:46.970 --> 00:47:49.400
culturally competent communications

00:47:49.400 --> 00:47:50.960
is a really important part,

00:47:50.960 --> 00:47:53.900
which I think leads into the
second part to your question;

00:47:53.900 --> 00:47:56.360
that challenge is bigger for us this year.

00:47:56.360 --> 00:47:59.060
Last year, there was a
very significant degree

00:47:59.060 --> 00:48:02.297
of resource available for marketing.

00:48:04.220 --> 00:48:07.010
That sounds like the wrong
phrase, but you know what I mean.

00:48:07.010 --> 00:48:08.270
- [Richard] Encouraging
people to come forward.

00:48:08.270 --> 00:48:09.350
- [Steve] Yeah.

00:48:09.350 --> 00:48:12.740
Providing information, building awareness,

00:48:12.740 --> 00:48:15.500
highlighting risk, highlighting the offer.

00:48:15.500 --> 00:48:17.060
And you don't just do
that through social media,

00:48:17.060 --> 00:48:19.853
and the front pages of mainstream media.

00:48:20.990 --> 00:48:23.300
That challenge is exacerbated this year,

00:48:23.300 --> 00:48:27.440
with a much lower budget
for those activities,

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:30.470
which was a decision made
outside of NHS England,

00:48:30.470 --> 00:48:32.420
but one that we have to work within,

00:48:32.420 --> 00:48:34.970
and then have to work harder at addressing

00:48:34.970 --> 00:48:37.157
with local NHS communications staff.

00:48:37.157 --> 00:48:38.630
- [Richard] Thank you Steve.
David, do you want to pick up?

00:48:38.630 --> 00:48:40.790
- [David] Just touch on two
further things very briefly.

00:48:40.790 --> 00:48:42.830
Firstly, on elective, it is worth noting

00:48:42.830 --> 00:48:43.940
since the last time we met,

00:48:43.940 --> 00:48:46.820
that we've met our first
delivery plan target,

00:48:46.820 --> 00:48:49.880
virtually eliminating all
of the 104 week waiters

00:48:49.880 --> 00:48:52.790
by treating over 70,000 people

00:48:52.790 --> 00:48:55.160
who had already tripped 104 weeks.

00:48:55.160 --> 00:48:56.900
We would've done so if
we hadn't been treated.

00:48:56.900 --> 00:49:00.410
So, that's a fantastic
effort by all concerned.

00:49:00.410 --> 00:49:02.420
Of course, we now move
on to the next thing,

00:49:02.420 --> 00:49:03.920
which the 78 week waiters,

00:49:03.920 --> 00:49:06.470
of which there are just over 50,000,

00:49:06.470 --> 00:49:07.940
coming back to your opening remarks,

00:49:07.940 --> 00:49:09.680
which are the majority of those people

00:49:09.680 --> 00:49:12.110
do sit in a non-admitted pathway,

00:49:12.110 --> 00:49:13.670
i.e. the outpatient department,

00:49:13.670 --> 00:49:16.430
so our absolute focus for the next stage

00:49:16.430 --> 00:49:18.530
will be on that 80% who will be

00:49:18.530 --> 00:49:20.510
in the outpatient department.

00:49:20.510 --> 00:49:23.630
Coming back to the references
about the impact of COVID,

00:49:23.630 --> 00:49:25.640
we have got progressively better

00:49:25.640 --> 00:49:27.380
at keeping the elective show on the road

00:49:27.380 --> 00:49:29.150
through each wave of COVID.

00:49:29.150 --> 00:49:30.560
And we need to do everything we can

00:49:30.560 --> 00:49:32.270
to keep those green pathways open,

00:49:32.270 --> 00:49:36.113
to ensure that we keep this
focus on elective recovery.

00:49:36.980 --> 00:49:39.170
The other area, of course,
the final area I'll touch on,

00:49:39.170 --> 00:49:40.610
is urgent and emergency care,

00:49:40.610 --> 00:49:42.890
which is under significant pressure,

00:49:42.890 --> 00:49:45.590
and certainly the advent
of the fourth wave of COVID

00:49:45.590 --> 00:49:48.020
is putting additional
pressure into the system.

00:49:48.020 --> 00:49:50.930
As Amanda said, we have
written out to all ICBs

00:49:50.930 --> 00:49:52.940
who brought their plans back to us.

00:49:52.940 --> 00:49:55.490
We have seen just over one
and a half thousand beds

00:49:55.490 --> 00:49:58.160
already open since the plan was sent out,

00:49:58.160 --> 00:50:00.290
and we continue to work with those trusts

00:50:00.290 --> 00:50:01.580
who are most challenged

00:50:01.580 --> 00:50:03.590
in the ambulance handover performance.

00:50:03.590 --> 00:50:06.320
I think I mentioned it's six
trusts in the first cohort

00:50:06.320 --> 00:50:08.243
and four in the second cohort.

00:50:10.070 --> 00:50:11.510
So, I'll pause there, Richard.

00:50:11.510 --> 00:50:13.550
Actually quite happy to
take questions on that,

00:50:13.550 --> 00:50:16.400
or indeed anything else in the report.

00:50:16.400 --> 00:50:18.230
- Any further?

00:50:18.230 --> 00:50:19.820
Munir, do you want to go, and then Andrew,

00:50:19.820 --> 00:50:21.306
and then I'm going to
move us on to Julian,

00:50:21.306 --> 00:50:23.840
because the financial update's
also extremely important.

00:50:23.840 --> 00:50:24.680
- [Munir] So, I'm going to ask you

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:26.540
about cardiovascular disease to make sure

00:50:26.540 --> 00:50:29.150
that I have been reading your paper.

00:50:29.150 --> 00:50:30.300
- Are you ready, Steve?

00:50:31.310 --> 00:50:34.970
- So, it's great to see the
thrombolysis rates going up,

00:50:34.970 --> 00:50:37.850
and it highlights the importance

00:50:37.850 --> 00:50:42.200
of implementing technology
into clinical care pathway,

00:50:42.200 --> 00:50:44.570
and the artificial
intelligence tools increasing

00:50:44.570 --> 00:50:48.730
from such a low level
to 77% within a year,

00:50:48.730 --> 00:50:51.260
or just over a year is
a fantastic achievement.

00:50:51.260 --> 00:50:54.890
So, just to give people watching an idea

00:50:54.890 --> 00:50:59.540
of how much time that's
saving in terms of improving

00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:01.310
the numbers of people
who can get thrombolized

00:51:01.310 --> 00:51:03.610
within the four hour
time window that we have?

00:51:07.661 --> 00:51:08.720
- [Andrew] I don't know if
you want to add anything?

00:51:08.720 --> 00:51:11.180
I think that's a comment as
much as a question, isn't it?

00:51:11.180 --> 00:51:13.310
- [Steve] Other than to
say we are seeing recovery

00:51:13.310 --> 00:51:15.770
in thrombolysis rates, as you say, Munir.

00:51:15.770 --> 00:51:19.430
We also need to see greater
progress in thrombectomy rates,

00:51:19.430 --> 00:51:23.000
which is where clots
are not just dissolved,

00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:27.320
but are actually removed
from the blood vessels

00:51:27.320 --> 00:51:30.560
in the brain where they've
lodged that cause a stroke.

00:51:30.560 --> 00:51:32.630
We have a long term plan ambition on that,

00:51:32.630 --> 00:51:35.570
which got disrupted during the pandemic,

00:51:35.570 --> 00:51:37.640
but we are getting back
on track with that,

00:51:37.640 --> 00:51:39.590
in a renewed focus.

00:51:39.590 --> 00:51:43.370
Of course, it's really important
that if you have symptoms

00:51:43.370 --> 00:51:45.170
of a stroke and indeed a heart attack,

00:51:45.170 --> 00:51:48.290
that you get to hospitals
as quickly as possible.

00:51:48.290 --> 00:51:50.990
So, that's why we have a really huge focus

00:51:50.990 --> 00:51:52.250
on ambulance response times,

00:51:52.250 --> 00:51:54.320
and we're working with
our ambulance services

00:51:54.320 --> 00:51:58.460
to see how we can particularly
improve responsiveness

00:51:58.460 --> 00:52:00.380
to strokes and heart attacks,

00:52:00.380 --> 00:52:02.663
and conditions with that severity.

00:52:03.560 --> 00:52:08.393
A real focus on ensuring
that we drive up those rates

00:52:08.393 --> 00:52:13.393
of life saving, and indeed
life changing treatments

00:52:15.830 --> 00:52:18.173
to treat stroke.

00:52:21.020 --> 00:52:22.040
- Andrew?

00:52:22.040 --> 00:52:23.750
- [Andrew] Just a couple of points, David.

00:52:23.750 --> 00:52:27.530
So, it's great to see some
very, very early progress

00:52:27.530 --> 00:52:28.700
around planning for winter.

00:52:28.700 --> 00:52:32.540
It's the first, for my
five years on the board,

00:52:32.540 --> 00:52:37.130
and given that we've
got the 7,000 extra beds

00:52:37.130 --> 00:52:38.360
as a cornerstone.

00:52:38.360 --> 00:52:42.110
We've got 1500, you've just
mentioned already commissioned.

00:52:42.110 --> 00:52:45.290
When do you think all
7,000 will be in place?

00:52:45.290 --> 00:52:48.410
And then the second bit
is around the 500 million

00:52:48.410 --> 00:52:52.760
Secretary of State announced
for earlier discharge.

00:52:52.760 --> 00:52:55.820
What kind of impact do you
think that's going to make?

00:52:55.820 --> 00:52:56.660
- [Steve] Right. Okay.

00:52:56.660 --> 00:52:58.133
So, I'll take those.

00:53:00.020 --> 00:53:01.770
What's the first one again, Andrew?

00:53:02.903 --> 00:53:03.736
- [Andrew] The beds.

00:53:03.736 --> 00:53:04.569
- [Steve] That's right.

00:53:04.569 --> 00:53:05.402
- [Andrew] Are we going to get are 7,000?

00:53:05.402 --> 00:53:06.344
- [Steve] That's fine.

00:53:06.344 --> 00:53:07.280
So, we've just received
the returns back from each

00:53:07.280 --> 00:53:08.840
of the ICBs,

00:53:08.840 --> 00:53:11.780
and the there are one or
two outstanding actually,

00:53:11.780 --> 00:53:14.000
but it looks like we will model through

00:53:14.000 --> 00:53:17.840
to have those beds open, 6,000
of the 7,000 by Christmas,

00:53:17.840 --> 00:53:21.080
the other 1000 by the end
of the financial year.

00:53:21.080 --> 00:53:23.810
Now, within that, there
is also a certain gearing

00:53:23.810 --> 00:53:27.230
between what we would
recognise as an actual bed,

00:53:27.230 --> 00:53:29.390
with a metal bed with some
oxygen at the top of it,

00:53:29.390 --> 00:53:31.460
and virtual beds and others.

00:53:31.460 --> 00:53:35.060
But that is what the phasing
looks like at the moment.

00:53:35.060 --> 00:53:37.520
Now, coming back to Amanda's
opening remarks, Andrew,

00:53:37.520 --> 00:53:41.300
we need to be still looking
to go further, faster, higher,

00:53:41.300 --> 00:53:42.500
as I keep describing it,

00:53:42.500 --> 00:53:44.270
and to see whether or not we will find

00:53:44.270 --> 00:53:46.640
some additional capacity
over and above that,

00:53:46.640 --> 00:53:49.790
should the pressure on the
system exceed those needs.

00:53:49.790 --> 00:53:52.820
But that's the modelling through
as it sits at the moment.

00:53:52.820 --> 00:53:55.100
So, in terms of your latter point,

00:53:55.100 --> 00:53:57.740
I don't think we know
yet, is the short answer.

00:53:57.740 --> 00:54:01.460
We were aware that the
money has been announced,

00:54:01.460 --> 00:54:02.293
if you like.

00:54:02.293 --> 00:54:05.180
It's the 500 million, and at
the moment we're discussing

00:54:05.180 --> 00:54:06.590
how and where that will be allocated.

00:54:06.590 --> 00:54:09.260
I should emphasise
that's not our decision,

00:54:09.260 --> 00:54:10.820
that's department's decision,

00:54:10.820 --> 00:54:12.970
but we are involved in
those conversations.

00:54:15.260 --> 00:54:16.200
- [Richard] I'm going
to hand to Julian now,

00:54:16.200 --> 00:54:19.580
but I do think that what
I find really helpful

00:54:19.580 --> 00:54:21.290
is when we break the conversation down

00:54:21.290 --> 00:54:23.000
into really tangible actions.

00:54:23.000 --> 00:54:25.340
So, whether it's the number of
beds which are physical beds

00:54:25.340 --> 00:54:27.830
or other provision,
whether it's a contract

00:54:27.830 --> 00:54:31.580
with St. John's Ambulance,
either to provide 850 more staff,

00:54:31.580 --> 00:54:34.310
I think across ambulance services,

00:54:34.310 --> 00:54:36.290
where there's lots of work
going on in primary care,

00:54:36.290 --> 00:54:38.630
whether it's the work
done on category two calls

00:54:38.630 --> 00:54:41.300
around falls of the elderly.

00:54:41.300 --> 00:54:43.400
And so, what are the real tangible actions

00:54:43.400 --> 00:54:47.063
that the National Health
Service is introducing?

00:54:48.980 --> 00:54:50.600
We may be sitting here at NHS England,

00:54:50.600 --> 00:54:52.160
but our colleagues throughout the systems

00:54:52.160 --> 00:54:53.930
are introducing, it
says, tangible actions,

00:54:53.930 --> 00:54:56.630
which I like to hear more
about all the time actually.

00:54:57.800 --> 00:54:58.633
Julian.

00:54:59.600 --> 00:55:00.433
- [Julian] On the financial-

00:55:00.433 --> 00:55:01.505
- Cometh the moment cometh-

00:55:01.505 --> 00:55:04.410
- So, I think it's
worth updating the board

00:55:05.390 --> 00:55:08.690
on both the position as
we find it this year,

00:55:08.690 --> 00:55:10.490
and the sort of financial outlook,

00:55:10.490 --> 00:55:15.170
as we are developing planning
guidance and expectations

00:55:15.170 --> 00:55:17.603
for next year and the year beyond.

00:55:19.310 --> 00:55:21.893
On the position on this year,

00:55:23.690 --> 00:55:25.073
at the end of month four,

00:55:29.330 --> 00:55:34.160
overall, we are in aggregate
about 200 million off plan.

00:55:34.160 --> 00:55:37.400
That is largely because we
are seeing some deterioration

00:55:37.400 --> 00:55:39.683
in the provider position.

00:55:40.550 --> 00:55:45.550
Systems overall are actually,
from the commissioner side,

00:55:45.740 --> 00:55:50.273
net basically holding to plan.

00:55:52.070 --> 00:55:57.070
Clearly, we need to see what happens.

00:55:57.680 --> 00:55:59.090
Our providers, as we have discussed

00:55:59.090 --> 00:56:00.890
from the operational perspective,

00:56:00.890 --> 00:56:03.890
are under enormous pressure.

00:56:03.890 --> 00:56:07.343
In particular, as David
and Amanda have said,

00:56:09.440 --> 00:56:12.920
we are now into our I
think third COVID wave

00:56:12.920 --> 00:56:15.320
during this financial year.

00:56:15.320 --> 00:56:17.750
Staff sickness absence is
clearly materially higher

00:56:17.750 --> 00:56:22.400
than we had hoped or assumed
or asked people to plan for.

00:56:22.400 --> 00:56:26.450
That is driving clearly much
higher temporary staffing costs

00:56:26.450 --> 00:56:29.300
as we seek to deal with the pressure,

00:56:29.300 --> 00:56:34.300
and as providers continue to
deal with emergency demand

00:56:35.600 --> 00:56:36.590
in particular.

00:56:36.590 --> 00:56:38.630
But actually, the cost of elective demand

00:56:38.630 --> 00:56:41.673
is also as a result higher,
because you are having

00:56:41.673 --> 00:56:46.673
to rely on out of normal session costs,

00:56:49.220 --> 00:56:51.200
in order to maintain activity.

00:56:51.200 --> 00:56:54.890
Just as we've said in
the operational picture,

00:56:54.890 --> 00:56:56.960
we are maintaining much higher levels

00:56:56.960 --> 00:57:01.280
of other elective planned
care through this year

00:57:01.280 --> 00:57:06.280
than compared to what was
done, let's say, in '21, '22,

00:57:06.440 --> 00:57:08.000
as we saw COVID waves,

00:57:08.000 --> 00:57:12.740
which reflects actually just
how hard people are working.

00:57:12.740 --> 00:57:14.303
The position is very tight.

00:57:17.120 --> 00:57:19.400
At the moment, we are still forecasting

00:57:19.400 --> 00:57:21.200
to come in on budget.

00:57:21.200 --> 00:57:23.783
There is clearly risk there.

00:57:26.960 --> 00:57:28.970
We recognise, as has been said,

00:57:28.970 --> 00:57:30.770
and welcome the additional
funding that is going

00:57:30.770 --> 00:57:32.690
into discharge and social care.

00:57:32.690 --> 00:57:35.630
500 million pounds this winter.

00:57:35.630 --> 00:57:37.310
I think, in essence, this
is also the government

00:57:37.310 --> 00:57:41.027
recognising the need to step back up

00:57:41.027 --> 00:57:43.820
the hospital discharge scheme
or some variation thereof,

00:57:43.820 --> 00:57:46.490
that we had through the
course of last year,

00:57:46.490 --> 00:57:48.833
given the pressures that we are seeing.

00:57:50.570 --> 00:57:54.800
So watching closely, managing tightly,

00:57:54.800 --> 00:57:59.660
implementing the cuts that
we said we would need to

00:57:59.660 --> 00:58:02.450
following the pay round.

00:58:02.450 --> 00:58:05.390
Our contribution to living with COVID

00:58:05.390 --> 00:58:09.560
as it affects both technology
investment in particular,

00:58:09.560 --> 00:58:11.210
some of the growth and diagnostic capacity

00:58:11.210 --> 00:58:12.293
that we had planned.

00:58:14.420 --> 00:58:18.380
And then, of course,
the steps we are taking

00:58:18.380 --> 00:58:22.430
within this organisation to
basically freeze recruitment,

00:58:22.430 --> 00:58:27.140
reduce costs as we step
through the merger programme.

00:58:27.140 --> 00:58:30.320
So, lots going on. It is very tight.

00:58:30.320 --> 00:58:33.083
We will continue to monitor it closely.

00:58:34.790 --> 00:58:37.943
Now clearly, as we look
ahead to next year,

00:58:40.700 --> 00:58:44.030
the position again is fairly uncertain,

00:58:44.030 --> 00:58:45.083
and very pressured.

00:58:46.520 --> 00:58:50.520
Worth of course clocking that
we came into the pandemic

00:58:51.770 --> 00:58:56.360
with 10 years of funding
significantly below the level

00:58:56.360 --> 00:59:01.360
of the historic trend rate
of growth in health spending.

00:59:01.670 --> 00:59:03.620
It meant that we went into the pandemic

00:59:03.620 --> 00:59:08.620
with reasonably little or no resilience.

00:59:09.440 --> 00:59:14.440
If you compared us at
19/20 for nurses, doctors,

00:59:15.260 --> 00:59:17.180
acute beds per head of population,

00:59:17.180 --> 00:59:19.670
compared to most of the
Western European countries,

00:59:19.670 --> 00:59:22.980
we were clearly right down at the low end

00:59:24.410 --> 00:59:27.620
of the league table in the benchmarks.

00:59:27.620 --> 00:59:30.650
As a result, as we went into the pandemic,

00:59:30.650 --> 00:59:32.420
as we had to deal with the demands,

00:59:32.420 --> 00:59:36.410
the almost complete reorganisation
of care in hospitals,

00:59:36.410 --> 00:59:40.010
the staffing demands,
higher sickness absence,

00:59:40.010 --> 00:59:42.320
the various other measures,
nightingale hospitals,

00:59:42.320 --> 00:59:47.320
the use of the IS, the vaccine
programme testing, PPE cost.

00:59:48.890 --> 00:59:52.760
I mean, we clearly saw
quite a big increase

00:59:52.760 --> 00:59:56.363
in costs within the NHS.

00:59:57.560 --> 00:59:59.633
We have been working that out.

01:00:00.560 --> 01:00:05.337
This year, in total funding for the NHS,

01:00:06.470 --> 01:00:11.470
it was planned to be reduced
in real terms by about 1.4%.

01:00:13.940 --> 01:00:16.970
That includes, of course,

01:00:16.970 --> 01:00:21.530
further reductions in the funding
we were provided for COVID

01:00:22.820 --> 01:00:25.730
on the basis that we
would be able to change

01:00:25.730 --> 01:00:27.950
infection prevention control arrangements,

01:00:27.950 --> 01:00:32.540
and indeed would see
significantly lower COVID demand.

01:00:32.540 --> 01:00:35.060
We are still trying to
work those costs out.

01:00:35.060 --> 01:00:39.890
In total, the efficiency
ask of systems this year

01:00:39.890 --> 01:00:44.890
was about 5%, 5.6 billion
pounds in cash terms.

01:00:45.710 --> 01:00:47.603
I mean, that is very stretching.

01:00:48.560 --> 01:00:50.510
Clearly, some of the
assumptions that we were asked

01:00:50.510 --> 01:00:53.000
to make when we did the
planning in the SI last year

01:00:53.000 --> 01:00:55.010
have clearly not borne
fruit, in particular,

01:00:55.010 --> 01:00:56.450
as we've discussed,

01:00:56.450 --> 01:00:59.820
we've seen higher numbers of
COVID patients in hospital

01:01:00.770 --> 01:01:03.560
over the course of this last six months

01:01:03.560 --> 01:01:06.390
than actually the
equivalent six month period

01:01:07.520 --> 01:01:09.983
at any other point during the pandemic.

01:01:11.540 --> 01:01:13.840
As we've also discussed
an operational update,

01:01:14.690 --> 01:01:18.110
we've also seen a decline in
the domiciliary care workforce

01:01:18.110 --> 01:01:19.160
over the last 12 months,

01:01:19.160 --> 01:01:21.920
which is making it harder
to discharge patients

01:01:21.920 --> 01:01:23.960
back to their homes.

01:01:23.960 --> 01:01:26.723
That has driven much higher bed occupancy.

01:01:27.620 --> 01:01:31.940
We currently have over
7,000 patients in hospital

01:01:31.940 --> 01:01:34.910
for longer than seven days
than we had pre-pandemic.

01:01:34.910 --> 01:01:38.750
So, that's part of both the
operational and cost pressure

01:01:38.750 --> 01:01:41.360
we are dealing with within the NHS now.

01:01:41.360 --> 01:01:46.360
But in addition to that
stretching assumption,

01:01:47.960 --> 01:01:50.870
as we have discussed in previous boards,

01:01:50.870 --> 01:01:54.590
we have also had to absorb
indeed higher inflation costs.

01:01:54.590 --> 01:01:58.640
So, the real terms effect
is more significant.

01:01:58.640 --> 01:02:01.853
So we've put an extra one
and a half billion pounds in,

01:02:03.290 --> 01:02:06.440
and we have had to find
about another one and a half

01:02:06.440 --> 01:02:11.440
billion pounds to deal with a contribution

01:02:11.480 --> 01:02:13.763
to the pay settlement,

01:02:15.319 --> 01:02:18.780
a contribution to the
broader testing costs

01:02:20.780 --> 01:02:22.610
that are being managed by the department,

01:02:22.610 --> 01:02:25.790
and certain other responsibilities
transferred to us,

01:02:25.790 --> 01:02:29.510
which we have had to
absorb without funding.

01:02:29.510 --> 01:02:33.920
So, it has been a pretty
pressured situation.

01:02:33.920 --> 01:02:38.920
Now, when we did the
SR agreement last year,

01:02:39.650 --> 01:02:41.315
we committed though to deliver

01:02:41.315 --> 01:02:43.490
further significant efficiencies.

01:02:43.490 --> 01:02:47.390
If you take the 5.6
billion that I outlined,

01:02:47.390 --> 01:02:51.230
plus about a further
3.6 billion next year,

01:02:51.230 --> 01:02:55.640
and a further 2.8 billion the year after,

01:02:55.640 --> 01:02:58.670
in total we had committed to delivering

01:02:58.670 --> 01:03:02.870
around 12 billion pounds
of efficiency savings,

01:03:02.870 --> 01:03:07.170
which clearly includes
us basically reducing

01:03:08.600 --> 01:03:10.310
in particular the extra COVID costs,

01:03:10.310 --> 01:03:12.290
which we have received
from around 5 billion,

01:03:12.290 --> 01:03:15.320
to about half a billion
by the end of '24 '25.

01:03:15.320 --> 01:03:18.710
I mean, basically, that we
would work out over three years

01:03:18.710 --> 01:03:22.133
the extra cost pressures that we-

01:03:26.269 --> 01:03:27.140
- [Richard] Need to compensate for.

01:03:27.140 --> 01:03:31.400
- Yeah. That we had during COVID.

01:03:31.400 --> 01:03:34.490
In addition, even if you
take the extra cost pressures

01:03:34.490 --> 01:03:37.313
that we have had to absorb this year,

01:03:38.390 --> 01:03:42.113
and at the moment, we
at least have to think

01:03:42.113 --> 01:03:46.190
that those are likely to
be recurrent pressures,

01:03:46.190 --> 01:03:48.080
although that is a
conversation we are having

01:03:48.080 --> 01:03:50.330
with the department and government,

01:03:50.330 --> 01:03:52.440
we could be seeing total cost pressures

01:03:53.538 --> 01:03:57.590
somewhere around 14 or
more billion pounds,

01:03:57.590 --> 01:03:59.340
savings in total that we have to make.

01:03:59.340 --> 01:04:01.700
- [Richard] By '24, '25.
- By '24 '25,

01:04:01.700 --> 01:04:06.700
which in essence is 10%
of our nominal cost base.

01:04:07.130 --> 01:04:09.780
Now, we have been putting
in place all the plans

01:04:10.730 --> 01:04:12.560
to deal with that,

01:04:12.560 --> 01:04:14.052
but that's going to give us

01:04:14.052 --> 01:04:17.240
and the government
reasonably stringent choices

01:04:17.240 --> 01:04:19.580
about where investment can go.

01:04:19.580 --> 01:04:21.320
I mean, working out how we can do that

01:04:21.320 --> 01:04:26.320
whilst delivering the reduction
in the elective long waits.

01:04:26.540 --> 01:04:28.520
The improvements in cancer performance,

01:04:28.520 --> 01:04:31.490
and indeed how we deal with
the emergency pressures,

01:04:31.490 --> 01:04:34.730
and how we improve access to primary care,

01:04:34.730 --> 01:04:38.843
as well as the investment in
mental health and other issues.

01:04:39.770 --> 01:04:42.560
Clearly, the confounding
factor though right now

01:04:42.560 --> 01:04:46.790
is the potential ongoing
inflation pressure.

01:04:46.790 --> 01:04:48.800
Now, there is clearly massive uncertainty

01:04:48.800 --> 01:04:51.140
as to exactly what that will be,

01:04:51.140 --> 01:04:53.815
but if you were to take the
Bank of England's last forecast

01:04:53.815 --> 01:04:56.660
they were forecasting
around 9% CPI next year,

01:04:56.660 --> 01:04:58.897
even if it then fell back down to about 2%

01:04:59.997 --> 01:05:01.163
the year after that.

01:05:02.090 --> 01:05:06.470
And if as a result of
that level of inflation

01:05:06.470 --> 01:05:10.220
and indeed as you look at
benchmark private sector earnings,

01:05:10.220 --> 01:05:14.390
you were to see a pay settlement
next year for NHS staff,

01:05:14.390 --> 01:05:17.783
or indeed the broader public sector,

01:05:19.040 --> 01:05:21.260
similar to that we have seen this year.

01:05:21.260 --> 01:05:23.690
Remembering we were
funded on a 2% inflation,

01:05:23.690 --> 01:05:26.240
2% paid settlement basis,

01:05:26.240 --> 01:05:28.100
we could see further cost pressures

01:05:28.100 --> 01:05:30.200
of about six to 7 billion pounds.

01:05:30.200 --> 01:05:31.100
- [Richard] In addition to the 14-

01:05:31.100 --> 01:05:35.090
- In addition to the, let's
say around 14 billion pounds

01:05:35.090 --> 01:05:38.420
we're already thinking we
might have to be consuming.

01:05:38.420 --> 01:05:41.646
At that point you're clearly not talking

01:05:41.646 --> 01:05:44.330
about further efficiencies.

01:05:44.330 --> 01:05:46.880
We will be having to consider,

01:05:46.880 --> 01:05:49.110
and talking through with the government

01:05:50.000 --> 01:05:53.183
what the options would be
to manage that pressure,

01:05:54.800 --> 01:05:56.990
and if we had to manage it
within the nominal budget

01:05:56.990 --> 01:05:58.520
that we have.

01:05:58.520 --> 01:06:02.180
I mean, clearly, you have
to completely revisit

01:06:02.180 --> 01:06:06.680
investment in cancer,
mental health, primary care,

01:06:06.680 --> 01:06:08.900
the increase in diagnostic capacity,

01:06:08.900 --> 01:06:10.880
and you would have to
look at what it meant

01:06:10.880 --> 01:06:13.493
in total for what the NHS could deliver.

01:06:15.852 --> 01:06:17.453
- [Richard] If I summarise,

01:06:19.880 --> 01:06:22.700
cogent presentation of
a list of the pressures

01:06:22.700 --> 01:06:24.230
that we face,

01:06:24.230 --> 01:06:26.660
the things that come out at me are,

01:06:26.660 --> 01:06:31.040
by 2024-25, we're looking around a 10%

01:06:31.040 --> 01:06:33.653
of running operating costs.

01:06:34.597 --> 01:06:37.790
It's not saving pressure
that we have to find.

01:06:37.790 --> 01:06:40.160
And in addition to that,

01:06:40.160 --> 01:06:42.140
potentially another six or seven billion,

01:06:42.140 --> 01:06:43.640
depending upon the model's assumption

01:06:43.640 --> 01:06:48.570
for the stickiness of inflation
working its way through.

01:06:50.810 --> 01:06:52.760
And if that is what transpires,

01:06:52.760 --> 01:06:54.290
then clearly there are conversations

01:06:54.290 --> 01:06:57.470
we'll just need to have, in
terms of making presentations

01:06:57.470 --> 01:07:01.430
to government about various
options for their consideration.

01:07:01.430 --> 01:07:03.500
But can I just pause and see
if anyone has any comment?

01:07:03.500 --> 01:07:05.607
- [Wol] Can I get a bit
further on that, Richard?

01:07:05.607 --> 01:07:07.730
I mean Julian is a very cogent,

01:07:07.730 --> 01:07:10.100
very sobering presentation for the board.

01:07:10.100 --> 01:07:12.020
The pressures you're talking about,

01:07:12.020 --> 01:07:15.320
if I look across the private
sector, they're present there.

01:07:15.320 --> 01:07:17.330
Other parts of the
economy facing all this.

01:07:17.330 --> 01:07:19.037
But I think it's important as a board,

01:07:19.037 --> 01:07:22.130
and your call Richard,
as Chair, whether we

01:07:22.130 --> 01:07:25.133
as non-exec directors in particular,

01:07:26.180 --> 01:07:29.300
try and understand what the menu of things

01:07:29.300 --> 01:07:30.770
that the management were thinking about

01:07:30.770 --> 01:07:32.510
in their discussions with government,

01:07:32.510 --> 01:07:34.730
because there was a
long term plan set out,

01:07:34.730 --> 01:07:37.340
which we all signed up
to as board members.

01:07:37.340 --> 01:07:39.770
Clearly, the environment's changed.

01:07:39.770 --> 01:07:42.140
So, I'm just wondering whether
there's another deep dive

01:07:42.140 --> 01:07:43.490
or look at that.
- Yeah.

01:07:43.490 --> 01:07:46.008
- Which is really, really important.

01:07:46.008 --> 01:07:47.075
- And we've talked a bit before,

01:07:47.075 --> 01:07:50.030
based around a range of scenarios,

01:07:50.030 --> 01:07:52.910
and then a listing of potential options.

01:07:52.910 --> 01:07:54.260
- [Wol] Where you go, what you do.

01:07:54.260 --> 01:07:55.940
- Yeah, where you go, what you do.

01:07:55.940 --> 01:07:57.186
But, Amanda you want to?

01:07:57.186 --> 01:07:58.019
- [Wol] Sorry.

01:07:58.019 --> 01:07:59.696
- The only point I wanted to draw out

01:07:59.696 --> 01:08:03.200
of Julian's again excellent
presentation was just to say,

01:08:03.200 --> 01:08:05.330
you might be thinking, "What
earth were we thinking,

01:08:05.330 --> 01:08:08.630
signing up to those
original efficiency goals?"

01:08:08.630 --> 01:08:11.720
But as Julian said, clearly
we were making assumptions

01:08:11.720 --> 01:08:13.160
about both what would happen with COVID,

01:08:13.160 --> 01:08:15.770
but also that it is right
that we challenge ourselves

01:08:15.770 --> 01:08:17.600
to be as efficient as possible,

01:08:17.600 --> 01:08:21.920
and that we spend every single
penny of public money well.

01:08:21.920 --> 01:08:24.200
So, although the underlying efficiency

01:08:24.200 --> 01:08:27.380
does feel pretty ambitious
when you look at it cold

01:08:27.380 --> 01:08:30.200
or when it's added up in the
way Julian just has described,

01:08:30.200 --> 01:08:34.403
I think our sense was it
wasn't wrong to strive

01:08:34.403 --> 01:08:39.260
to really drive as much
efficiency and productivity

01:08:39.260 --> 01:08:42.140
as we possibly could across the NHS.

01:08:42.140 --> 01:08:44.450
Set aside those very clear ambitions

01:08:44.450 --> 01:08:46.700
that we had signed up to
around elective recovery,

01:08:46.700 --> 01:08:47.600
et cetera.

01:08:47.600 --> 01:08:50.690
I think the thing that
is right to just call out

01:08:50.690 --> 01:08:53.460
in particular from the way
Julian's just described it

01:08:54.535 --> 01:08:55.400
as we look into next year,

01:08:55.400 --> 01:08:59.540
it is the unquantified as yet,

01:08:59.540 --> 01:09:02.240
because we don't know exactly
where this is going to land,

01:09:02.240 --> 01:09:05.300
but these unquantified
additional challenges

01:09:05.300 --> 01:09:08.240
from particularly inflation,

01:09:08.240 --> 01:09:10.280
which, along with everything else

01:09:10.280 --> 01:09:11.870
that we are now looking at,

01:09:11.870 --> 01:09:14.900
I think is the reason why
this feels quite different

01:09:14.900 --> 01:09:16.820
to where it did when we
were previously discussed.

01:09:16.820 --> 01:09:18.367
- I'm conscious of the time,
and I do need to move us on.

01:09:18.367 --> 01:09:21.440
It's a really important conversation.

01:09:21.440 --> 01:09:26.060
What we haven't really talked
about in this moment actually

01:09:26.060 --> 01:09:29.720
is often to get that
productivity requires investment,

01:09:29.720 --> 01:09:33.680
and if we think about capital,
there's a conversation,

01:09:33.680 --> 01:09:36.890
and discussion needed around
actually capital investment

01:09:36.890 --> 01:09:39.290
in the health service to
enable the productivity levels

01:09:39.290 --> 01:09:43.040
that we should absolutely,
ambitiously be targeting.

01:09:43.040 --> 01:09:44.150
Is there anything else you'd like to say

01:09:44.150 --> 01:09:45.593
before I move us on, Julian?

01:09:46.670 --> 01:09:47.503
- [Julian] No.

01:09:47.503 --> 01:09:49.430
Look, clearly as you said it's inflation.

01:09:49.430 --> 01:09:50.333
I would just flag:

01:09:51.500 --> 01:09:54.440
at the moment it also
depends on what happens

01:09:54.440 --> 01:09:56.480
with energy cost in particular.

01:09:56.480 --> 01:10:00.290
The business relief scheme
is a massive assistance.

01:10:00.290 --> 01:10:02.390
- [Richard] Yeah, huge.
- Along with the discharge

01:10:02.390 --> 01:10:05.600
funding this year, so
it'll depend upon that,

01:10:05.600 --> 01:10:06.893
and as I said clearly,

01:10:09.980 --> 01:10:12.770
decisions on pay for staff,

01:10:12.770 --> 01:10:15.560
when clearly we would
want to be seeing fair

01:10:15.560 --> 01:10:18.680
and reasonable rewards for staff,

01:10:18.680 --> 01:10:22.490
given what they're doing
and the living pressures

01:10:22.490 --> 01:10:23.480
they are facing.

01:10:23.480 --> 01:10:26.780
But those are all recommendations done

01:10:26.780 --> 01:10:27.920
by an independent body,

01:10:27.920 --> 01:10:30.170
and to be handled by government next year.

01:10:30.170 --> 01:10:31.003
- [Richard] Thank you.

01:10:31.003 --> 01:10:32.480
I am going to move us on,

01:10:32.480 --> 01:10:35.060
although it's a very important discussion

01:10:35.060 --> 01:10:36.380
just led us through, Julian,

01:10:36.380 --> 01:10:37.930
and thank you very much for the clarity

01:10:37.930 --> 01:10:39.710
of that presentation.

01:10:39.710 --> 01:10:41.360
- Sorry, I know you were
trying to move us on, Richard,

01:10:41.360 --> 01:10:43.670
but the only thing, just add
to what Julian said about pay,

01:10:43.670 --> 01:10:45.290
and I think this is the
conversation we had before,

01:10:45.290 --> 01:10:46.550
isn't it?

01:10:46.550 --> 01:10:48.410
Actually, it's an operational necessity,

01:10:48.410 --> 01:10:51.500
that we have staff to do
the work we're describing.

01:10:51.500 --> 01:10:54.050
So, I know we've rehearsed
this in detail before,

01:10:54.050 --> 01:10:55.473
but I think it's not an either or.

01:10:55.473 --> 01:10:56.933
- It is not. I agree.

01:10:58.400 --> 01:10:59.810
Can we move on?

01:10:59.810 --> 01:11:01.790
Ruth, you're going to
lead us into the maternity

01:11:01.790 --> 01:11:03.980
and neonatal services update.

01:11:03.980 --> 01:11:05.480
And you've been joined by
a number of colleagues.

01:11:05.480 --> 01:11:06.313
- [Ruth] I have.

01:11:06.313 --> 01:11:10.463
I've got colleagues of Jacqui
and Donald and Duncan with me.

01:11:11.540 --> 01:11:13.629
Part of the team.

01:11:13.629 --> 01:11:15.440
So, I'm going to assume
that the board members

01:11:15.440 --> 01:11:16.320
have read the paper,

01:11:16.320 --> 01:11:20.180
and I just want to bring
out just a few points.

01:11:20.180 --> 01:11:24.893
First of all is on the 22nd
of this month, no September,

01:11:26.090 --> 01:11:29.450
I had the opportunity,
with Jacqui actually,

01:11:29.450 --> 01:11:32.750
to go to the Baby Lifeline
Charity Conference,

01:11:32.750 --> 01:11:37.750
and heard Derek talk
about his grandson Harry,

01:11:37.760 --> 01:11:39.920
Kaylee about her daughter Pippa,

01:11:39.920 --> 01:11:44.557
Sarah and Jack about
their daughter Harriet,

01:11:44.557 --> 01:11:48.680
and listening to them and other families,

01:11:48.680 --> 01:11:51.410
it does continue to drive our work,

01:11:51.410 --> 01:11:54.440
and importantly our actions.

01:11:54.440 --> 01:11:57.290
So, we know that east Kent's report

01:11:57.290 --> 01:11:59.210
is being led by Dr. Bill Kirkup.

01:11:59.210 --> 01:12:02.480
It's now going to be
published on the 19th October.

01:12:02.480 --> 01:12:07.460
And we will of course bring
back this to the next board,

01:12:07.460 --> 01:12:11.270
and we will consider that
as a board in due course.

01:12:11.270 --> 01:12:13.430
We also, since the last board meeting,

01:12:13.430 --> 01:12:15.440
have got the Nottingham
University Hospitals

01:12:15.440 --> 01:12:17.900
independent review of maternity.

01:12:17.900 --> 01:12:21.230
That has now been launched
on the first of September,

01:12:21.230 --> 01:12:23.510
chaired by Donna Ockenden.

01:12:23.510 --> 01:12:27.860
David is the SLO, and I'm there
very much supporting David,

01:12:27.860 --> 01:12:29.810
working together on it.

01:12:29.810 --> 01:12:33.383
We expect that report
to take about 18 months.

01:12:34.460 --> 01:12:37.820
We will maintain an
absolute regular contact

01:12:37.820 --> 01:12:38.990
with the review team,

01:12:38.990 --> 01:12:41.480
and facilitate the learning into practise,

01:12:41.480 --> 01:12:44.360
both locally and if
there's anything nationally

01:12:44.360 --> 01:12:46.400
throughout the review.

01:12:46.400 --> 01:12:49.940
We won't just wait till the
end of the report period

01:12:49.940 --> 01:12:50.843
to do that.

01:12:52.190 --> 01:12:55.130
You'll know that we've committed
as a board and as a team

01:12:55.130 --> 01:12:58.070
to create a single delivery plan,

01:12:58.070 --> 01:13:02.420
taking the work of the
maternity transformation board,

01:13:02.420 --> 01:13:06.350
the Shrewsbury and
Telford Ockenden reports,

01:13:06.350 --> 01:13:09.260
and of course then, the East Kent report

01:13:09.260 --> 01:13:11.240
that's due out later in the month.

01:13:11.240 --> 01:13:15.620
We'll bring that back,
probably now into the new year.

01:13:15.620 --> 01:13:19.910
Given that we've had a delay
of the east Kent report.

01:13:19.910 --> 01:13:20.901
In the interest of time,

01:13:20.901 --> 01:13:22.940
I'm just going to hand over
to Donald for a few minutes,

01:13:22.940 --> 01:13:23.773
and then Jacqui.

01:13:24.984 --> 01:13:26.750
- [Richard] Going to highlight
that we assume the paper's

01:13:26.750 --> 01:13:28.160
read, but draw out the
points you really want-

01:13:28.160 --> 01:13:31.280
- [Donald] To make sure we
really highlight three points.

01:13:31.280 --> 01:13:33.350
One is that by the end of this year,

01:13:33.350 --> 01:13:36.650
maternal medicine networks
will be operational

01:13:36.650 --> 01:13:38.510
across all parts of the country,

01:13:38.510 --> 01:13:40.820
which means that the footprints agreed,

01:13:40.820 --> 01:13:44.933
the centres are agreed, funding
is going to a central team,

01:13:46.460 --> 01:13:50.190
to improve access of women
with medical complexity

01:13:51.140 --> 01:13:53.210
to good care.

01:13:53.210 --> 01:13:56.240
The things that we still need to do are

01:13:56.240 --> 01:14:00.320
to embed a new form of
physician, obstetric physicians,

01:14:00.320 --> 01:14:03.440
which we're training and
are envisaged as the leaders

01:14:03.440 --> 01:14:04.733
of these networks.

01:14:06.260 --> 01:14:09.860
Evaluating their success, very important.

01:14:09.860 --> 01:14:13.550
And finally, meeting the
targets around inequalities,

01:14:13.550 --> 01:14:15.260
which actually, we've discussed with Bola,

01:14:15.260 --> 01:14:18.920
and are a really important
aim for these networks

01:14:18.920 --> 01:14:21.410
to improve, rather than hinder,

01:14:21.410 --> 01:14:24.713
access of the women who
really need these services.

01:14:26.420 --> 01:14:29.210
It's good to see that
smoking rates have come down,

01:14:29.210 --> 01:14:30.810
so they're at their lowest rate.

01:14:33.080 --> 01:14:37.610
But despite that, stillbirth rates,

01:14:37.610 --> 01:14:40.433
which have been falling year by year,

01:14:41.570 --> 01:14:44.690
and last year's data suggested

01:14:44.690 --> 01:14:47.930
we're meeting the national trajectory

01:14:47.930 --> 01:14:49.793
around halving stillbirths,

01:14:51.530 --> 01:14:54.110
this year, the data just out from ONS

01:14:54.110 --> 01:14:56.723
shows that they have increased.

01:14:57.920 --> 01:14:59.510
That increase has happened, obviously,

01:14:59.510 --> 01:15:02.153
at the peak time of COVID.

01:15:03.560 --> 01:15:06.830
I think we would be very
cautious about identifying

01:15:06.830 --> 01:15:10.400
any particular cause of
that increase in stillbirth.

01:15:10.400 --> 01:15:13.880
But, of course, there is
the effect of COVID itself,

01:15:13.880 --> 01:15:18.290
and then the access of mums to services

01:15:18.290 --> 01:15:19.670
as a result of COVID.

01:15:19.670 --> 01:15:22.880
Women being anxious about
coming into services,

01:15:22.880 --> 01:15:24.980
and we know that services were running

01:15:24.980 --> 01:15:27.893
very, very depleted workforce levels.

01:15:28.820 --> 01:15:32.450
If that is all correct,
then one would anticipate

01:15:32.450 --> 01:15:37.340
that the stillbirth rates
will continue to fall

01:15:37.340 --> 01:15:38.870
in subsequent years.

01:15:38.870 --> 01:15:40.730
But I think we have to wait and see,

01:15:40.730 --> 01:15:44.210
and it does make us think
about the importance

01:15:44.210 --> 01:15:49.040
of vaccination here and
in this particular group,

01:15:49.040 --> 01:15:51.350
because it's mums who have severe COVID,

01:15:51.350 --> 01:15:54.380
and we know from last time
round that those were the ones

01:15:54.380 --> 01:15:55.430
who were not vaxxed-

01:15:55.430 --> 01:15:56.473
- [Richard] Thank you.

01:15:58.430 --> 01:15:59.520
- Thanks. Jacqui.

01:16:01.520 --> 01:16:03.350
- [Jacqui] Thank you.

01:16:03.350 --> 01:16:07.880
Just to reflect on the
publication of the long term plan,

01:16:07.880 --> 01:16:09.760
and prior to that, Better Births Report

01:16:09.760 --> 01:16:11.210
of the National Maternity Review,

01:16:11.210 --> 01:16:13.970
we had an ambition for continuity of care,

01:16:13.970 --> 01:16:16.010
for most women to have continuity of care.

01:16:16.010 --> 01:16:20.300
The reasons why are evidence-based,
to improve outcomes.

01:16:20.300 --> 01:16:23.690
The model actually improves
outcomes and experiences,

01:16:23.690 --> 01:16:26.497
for example reduces preterm birth by 24%,

01:16:26.497 --> 01:16:29.090
and is very much improving
outcomes for women

01:16:29.090 --> 01:16:32.600
from Black, Asian minority
ethnic backgrounds.

01:16:32.600 --> 01:16:36.230
However that model of care
requires safe staffing,

01:16:36.230 --> 01:16:37.850
and appropriate staffing,

01:16:37.850 --> 01:16:40.010
and because of the staffing challenges

01:16:40.010 --> 01:16:41.450
that we're experiencing,

01:16:41.450 --> 01:16:45.410
we wrote out to maternity
providers, to trusts,

01:16:45.410 --> 01:16:48.560
post the final Ockenden report,

01:16:48.560 --> 01:16:53.560
and also further to our
publication last year,

01:16:53.840 --> 01:16:56.960
asking services to look at safe staffing

01:16:56.960 --> 01:16:58.220
before further rollout.

01:16:58.220 --> 01:17:01.910
So, we wrote out to
services informing them

01:17:01.910 --> 01:17:05.660
that we were removing the
target for continuity,

01:17:05.660 --> 01:17:09.260
and that's been responsive
to their staffing challenges,

01:17:09.260 --> 01:17:10.820
removing the target,

01:17:10.820 --> 01:17:15.140
and suspensions where there
are no safe staffing levels.

01:17:15.140 --> 01:17:20.140
However, on a positive note,
we have invested 1.3 million

01:17:20.990 --> 01:17:25.490
over the forthcoming
year to support services,

01:17:25.490 --> 01:17:27.830
where there is safe staffing,

01:17:27.830 --> 01:17:30.620
to target continuity for those living

01:17:30.620 --> 01:17:35.300
in the most deprived areas,
particularly black brown women,

01:17:35.300 --> 01:17:38.600
and those from a socioeconomically
disadvantaged background

01:17:38.600 --> 01:17:40.040
and mixed ethnicity women.

01:17:40.040 --> 01:17:42.500
They're the women that Embrace tells us

01:17:42.500 --> 01:17:45.533
are more likely to experience mortality.

01:17:46.820 --> 01:17:50.480
- [Richard] Can I just, because
Jacqui mentioned workforce,

01:17:50.480 --> 01:17:52.280
and I was reading the
section on workforce,

01:17:52.280 --> 01:17:56.420
and it's a mixed section,
because it has some paragraphs

01:17:56.420 --> 01:18:00.350
or points which are grounds for optimism.

01:18:00.350 --> 01:18:02.480
It finishes with the highest
ever number of students

01:18:02.480 --> 01:18:03.560
on undergraduate courses.

01:18:03.560 --> 01:18:07.190
It's the first year where the
impact of increased numbers

01:18:07.190 --> 01:18:09.740
of newly qualified
midwives enter the system.

01:18:09.740 --> 01:18:11.570
But at the same time,
that's the last paragraph.

01:18:11.570 --> 01:18:14.450
Before that, there's
an up and down of mood

01:18:14.450 --> 01:18:17.930
around how we're dealing with
the pressure on workforce,

01:18:17.930 --> 01:18:20.660
either obstetricians or midwives.

01:18:20.660 --> 01:18:21.620
Any comment?

01:18:21.620 --> 01:18:23.960
- [Jacqui] Duncan's just going
to finish off on workforce.

01:18:23.960 --> 01:18:25.040
- [Richard] Oh sorry.
- [Jacqui] Perfect segue.

01:18:25.040 --> 01:18:26.790
- [Richard] Go on, Duncan.

01:18:26.790 --> 01:18:28.190
- [Duncan] I think you're
probably right, chair,

01:18:28.190 --> 01:18:30.980
and the fact there's some
positive signs in here,

01:18:30.980 --> 01:18:34.160
but there's also some areas
which are more concerning.

01:18:34.160 --> 01:18:36.680
Obviously, the reduction
in midwives nationally

01:18:36.680 --> 01:18:39.590
I think is probably the
area of biggest concern.

01:18:39.590 --> 01:18:42.470
Particularly, our area of focus

01:18:42.470 --> 01:18:44.120
around retention of midwives.

01:18:44.120 --> 01:18:46.667
And so, there's a lot of work
going on working with Nabina

01:18:46.667 --> 01:18:50.720
and her team around retaining midwives.

01:18:50.720 --> 01:18:52.610
We've previously reported here,

01:18:52.610 --> 01:18:55.730
we funded the retention roles
into every organisation,

01:18:55.730 --> 01:18:59.600
to help support the work
around retaining staff.

01:18:59.600 --> 01:19:04.600
I think it's fair to say our
leaver rate has been higher

01:19:04.820 --> 01:19:07.160
than pre-pandemic and historic levels,

01:19:07.160 --> 01:19:09.050
but over the last couple
of months we have seen

01:19:09.050 --> 01:19:09.920
that stabilised.

01:19:09.920 --> 01:19:11.930
I think two months
probably too soon to say

01:19:11.930 --> 01:19:12.830
that that's a trend,

01:19:12.830 --> 01:19:15.053
but we'll need to
continue to monitor that,

01:19:15.053 --> 01:19:18.833
just to see if that continues
or what starts to come down.

01:19:19.910 --> 01:19:21.830
I think there is some positive news,

01:19:21.830 --> 01:19:24.950
in the fact that this is the
first year we'll see the impact

01:19:24.950 --> 01:19:28.070
of the increased numbers
of midwives in training,

01:19:28.070 --> 01:19:33.070
which HEE funded additional
training places three years ago.

01:19:34.520 --> 01:19:36.650
So, we should start to
see now year on year

01:19:36.650 --> 01:19:39.590
the number of midwives
qualifying increase.

01:19:39.590 --> 01:19:40.850
Over the next couple of months,

01:19:40.850 --> 01:19:43.670
as those newly qualified
midwives enter the profession,

01:19:43.670 --> 01:19:46.640
and enter our services,
we should see our numbers

01:19:46.640 --> 01:19:48.440
of midwives in practise increase.

01:19:48.440 --> 01:19:51.290
We obviously need to do
everything we can to retain them

01:19:51.290 --> 01:19:54.830
and to look after them as
they start in their careers.

01:19:54.830 --> 01:19:56.390
We have seen also an increase

01:19:56.390 --> 01:19:58.250
in our maternity support workers.

01:19:58.250 --> 01:19:59.900
And a lot of focused work around that,

01:19:59.900 --> 01:20:01.850
and we're just about to confirm

01:20:01.850 --> 01:20:03.110
some additional funding out to trusts

01:20:03.110 --> 01:20:04.370
to help support them with their work

01:20:04.370 --> 01:20:06.023
around maternity support workers.

01:20:07.460 --> 01:20:08.570
And then obstetrics.

01:20:08.570 --> 01:20:10.700
We've seen consulting
obstetric numbers increase,

01:20:10.700 --> 01:20:14.090
which is good, and we're
just in the process,

01:20:14.090 --> 01:20:17.180
also, we've had bids out to trusts,

01:20:17.180 --> 01:20:19.100
which we've had a fantastic response to,

01:20:19.100 --> 01:20:22.610
to help support additional
funding in every organisation

01:20:22.610 --> 01:20:24.710
for consultant leadership.

01:20:24.710 --> 01:20:26.870
Particularly mindful that actually,

01:20:26.870 --> 01:20:29.390
this isn't just about heads
and directors of midwifery.

01:20:29.390 --> 01:20:32.720
Running those services is a
combination of leadership.

01:20:32.720 --> 01:20:35.210
So, we hope that that will
support additional focus

01:20:35.210 --> 01:20:38.270
on driving some of this work forward.

01:20:38.270 --> 01:20:40.010
Lots of work going on around,

01:20:40.010 --> 01:20:41.930
we've got an international
recruitment programme

01:20:41.930 --> 01:20:42.763
for midwifery.

01:20:42.763 --> 01:20:44.540
We've had 147 midwives staffed

01:20:44.540 --> 01:20:46.850
so far this year internationally.

01:20:46.850 --> 01:20:50.120
We've got a return to midwifery
programme going on as well,

01:20:50.120 --> 01:20:53.483
which we're working with
colleagues in HEE on.

01:20:54.560 --> 01:20:57.380
Perhaps I'll pause there
and and take any questions.

01:20:57.380 --> 01:20:58.550
- [Richard] Yeah, I was
struck as you've got

01:20:58.550 --> 01:20:59.450
quite a high percentage.

01:20:59.450 --> 01:21:03.440
The increase in
obstetricians. It's 17, 18.

01:21:03.440 --> 01:21:06.880
Haven't done the maths
17 or 18% since July 21,

01:21:06.880 --> 01:21:08.090
if the numbers here are right.

01:21:08.090 --> 01:21:12.440
Was clearly struggling
more with increasing

01:21:12.440 --> 01:21:15.710
our midwifery workforce.

01:21:15.710 --> 01:21:17.390
I just wanted to note,
because I don't think

01:21:17.390 --> 01:21:19.100
we should lose sight of it,

01:21:19.100 --> 01:21:23.270
that just on the digital
electronic patient records

01:21:23.270 --> 01:21:24.446
attaching to this, clearly,

01:21:24.446 --> 01:21:26.960
I think we're taking a
view we're now no longer

01:21:26.960 --> 01:21:30.080
going to meet the commitment
that we had in the long term

01:21:30.080 --> 01:21:31.577
plan and we need to keep that under focus,

01:21:31.577 --> 01:21:34.310
and I can see we're revising the strategy

01:21:34.310 --> 01:21:37.850
about how we actually do
get some comprehensive

01:21:37.850 --> 01:21:39.140
electronic patient records.

01:21:39.140 --> 01:21:40.266
But you flagged that for us,

01:21:40.266 --> 01:21:42.380
and I think we should just
keep a weather eye on that.

01:21:42.380 --> 01:21:44.990
Now, a couple of minutes for Andrew.

01:21:44.990 --> 01:21:48.230
Did you have your hand
up? Thank you. And David?

01:21:48.230 --> 01:21:49.063
Okay, Andrew.

01:21:49.063 --> 01:21:50.690
- [Andrew] Okay.

01:21:50.690 --> 01:21:54.230
I think the team have come up
with some really great actions

01:21:54.230 --> 01:21:56.630
over the last two years,

01:21:56.630 --> 01:21:59.420
but I think this service is
still a very fragile one,

01:21:59.420 --> 01:22:02.150
and we mustn't think for
one moment as a board

01:22:02.150 --> 01:22:03.470
that it's all done and dusted,

01:22:03.470 --> 01:22:06.113
because we're some way from there.

01:22:07.220 --> 01:22:08.930
Two concerns I have.

01:22:08.930 --> 01:22:12.290
One is great news to see the
training numbers come through,

01:22:12.290 --> 01:22:16.580
but have we modelled the exit
rate of potential retirements?

01:22:16.580 --> 01:22:21.050
And do the two match, in
terms of a perfect cover

01:22:21.050 --> 01:22:24.020
going forward over a
five year horizon, say?

01:22:24.020 --> 01:22:26.120
And the other bit, just
picking upon Richard's point

01:22:26.120 --> 01:22:27.920
around consultant cover,

01:22:27.920 --> 01:22:31.040
all the evidence is that the
more consultant cover you have

01:22:31.040 --> 01:22:34.430
on the labour suite, the
better your outcomes.

01:22:34.430 --> 01:22:39.430
We've put 90 extra docs in
and I'm just curious to know

01:22:39.740 --> 01:22:44.450
to what extent are we hitting
90 hours labour suite cover

01:22:44.450 --> 01:22:46.880
at consultant level for your typical DGH

01:22:46.880 --> 01:22:48.170
that's delivering, say,

01:22:48.170 --> 01:22:50.270
three and a half thousand
babies every year.

01:22:50.270 --> 01:22:53.420
That, for me, is a
really important factor,

01:22:53.420 --> 01:22:54.920
because I think we've seen failure

01:22:54.920 --> 01:22:59.060
in not only the bigger units
but also the smaller units,

01:22:59.060 --> 01:23:01.070
and it's the smaller units that worry me,

01:23:01.070 --> 01:23:03.830
because they're more
difficult to recruit into.

01:23:03.830 --> 01:23:07.190
So, it'd be just good to
get people's reflections.

01:23:07.190 --> 01:23:08.023
- [Richard] Before we do that,

01:23:08.023 --> 01:23:09.340
can I bring David in for your comment?

01:23:09.340 --> 01:23:10.370
We do both together.

01:23:10.370 --> 01:23:13.310
- [David] So again, good
progress that's made,

01:23:13.310 --> 01:23:16.553
so thank you for Ruth and your team.

01:23:17.750 --> 01:23:19.430
In HEE, after the May board,

01:23:19.430 --> 01:23:21.650
we actually got some students in,

01:23:21.650 --> 01:23:25.527
both nurses and obstetricians
in training, and we said,

01:23:25.527 --> 01:23:27.197
"What is it like for you?"

01:23:28.370 --> 01:23:29.450
And actually what they told us

01:23:29.450 --> 01:23:33.140
was the importance of
protecting learning time.

01:23:33.140 --> 01:23:35.210
So, because of the workforce challenges,

01:23:35.210 --> 01:23:36.860
there is a real vicious circle that,

01:23:36.860 --> 01:23:39.020
if you don't protect the learning time,

01:23:39.020 --> 01:23:42.590
you're not giving the experience
for students in training,

01:23:42.590 --> 01:23:44.480
doctors in training.

01:23:44.480 --> 01:23:49.310
I just ask, as we work
through this programme,

01:23:49.310 --> 01:23:51.650
that we actually just
attend to that issue,

01:23:51.650 --> 01:23:54.260
because otherwise the training's

01:23:54.260 --> 01:23:55.670
going to be suboptimal for people.

01:23:55.670 --> 01:23:56.840
They're not going to get the training.

01:23:56.840 --> 01:23:58.430
They might have the hours,
but they're not going to get

01:23:58.430 --> 01:23:59.717
the quality of what they need to do,

01:23:59.717 --> 01:24:01.310
and I don't need a response.

01:24:01.310 --> 01:24:03.740
It's just putting down a
marker that I just think

01:24:03.740 --> 01:24:05.360
it's not just about numbers.

01:24:05.360 --> 01:24:07.953
It's about the experience
that those people get.

01:24:07.953 --> 01:24:09.100
- [Wol] One quick question-

01:24:09.100 --> 01:24:10.940
But I just don't think as a board

01:24:10.940 --> 01:24:15.940
we could not note the figures
which were paragraph five.

01:24:17.060 --> 01:24:19.867
900 million paid out
in past cases in 2021,

01:24:19.867 --> 01:24:21.923
and it costs 3 billion to run maternity.

01:24:21.923 --> 01:24:24.680
That doesn't work,
because that 900 million

01:24:24.680 --> 01:24:25.760
is only going one way.

01:24:25.760 --> 01:24:27.810
It's just very, very poor return overall.

01:24:28.670 --> 01:24:29.503
- [Richard] Thank you.

01:24:29.503 --> 01:24:31.970
Do you quickly want to
respond to Andrew's comment,

01:24:31.970 --> 01:24:33.980
because then I do need
to move us on actually.

01:24:33.980 --> 01:24:36.083
- [Ruth] Absolutely. I understand that.

01:24:37.119 --> 01:24:40.490
The workforce plan: we need
to keep the people we've got,

01:24:40.490 --> 01:24:44.180
we need to continue to make
sure we've got the demand

01:24:44.180 --> 01:24:45.980
and supply married up,

01:24:45.980 --> 01:24:48.890
and go for the over recruitment,
like Nabina and colleagues

01:24:48.890 --> 01:24:50.420
we were talking about this morning.

01:24:50.420 --> 01:24:51.680
Really important we do that.

01:24:51.680 --> 01:24:53.380
International recruitment
is only one tiny part

01:24:53.380 --> 01:24:54.590
of the solution.

01:24:54.590 --> 01:24:58.700
Retention and recruiting and
training our domestic supply

01:24:58.700 --> 01:25:02.240
has to be the biggest
part of our response.

01:25:02.240 --> 01:25:03.415
Labour suite cover,

01:25:03.415 --> 01:25:06.830
I'm delighted we've got
more doctors in there,

01:25:06.830 --> 01:25:08.390
but where they're working,

01:25:08.390 --> 01:25:10.130
and not just about their
labour suite cover,

01:25:10.130 --> 01:25:13.520
but actually are they there
supporting the midwives

01:25:13.520 --> 01:25:16.550
and working as a multidisciplinary team?

01:25:16.550 --> 01:25:17.383
Really important.

01:25:17.383 --> 01:25:20.523
And when I went to see Donald
and your colleagues in UCLH

01:25:20.523 --> 01:25:24.110
some six months ago, you
could see it working,

01:25:24.110 --> 01:25:26.000
but it doesn't work in all areas,

01:25:26.000 --> 01:25:27.740
and that's going to be important.

01:25:27.740 --> 01:25:29.690
Learning time, really important,

01:25:29.690 --> 01:25:32.297
and money is vitally important.

01:25:32.297 --> 01:25:35.480
And we know that a lot goes on litigation,

01:25:35.480 --> 01:25:39.230
and that's why, of
course, we must remember

01:25:39.230 --> 01:25:42.500
that the government did accept

01:25:42.500 --> 01:25:45.320
all of Donna Ockenden's recommendations.

01:25:45.320 --> 01:25:47.048
- [Richard] Thank you.
Thank you very much.

01:25:47.048 --> 01:25:49.700
I thank, Ruth, you and all
your colleagues actually,

01:25:49.700 --> 01:25:52.070
for that discussion.

01:25:52.070 --> 01:25:57.070
Can we move on to, Amanda,
access to primary care?

01:25:57.170 --> 01:25:58.700
- [Amanda] Thank you.

01:25:58.700 --> 01:26:00.830
I will take the paper as read,

01:26:00.830 --> 01:26:03.470
but I do want to just pull
out some important points

01:26:03.470 --> 01:26:04.303
in this paper.

01:26:04.303 --> 01:26:05.136
Thanks Richard.

01:26:07.730 --> 01:26:09.863
Despite the fact that primary care-

01:26:11.000 --> 01:26:13.070
- [Richard] I'm sorry.
Slightly more volume?

01:26:13.070 --> 01:26:13.903
- I will.

01:26:13.903 --> 01:26:15.440
Despite the fact that general practise

01:26:15.440 --> 01:26:17.330
particularly are delivering
more appointments

01:26:17.330 --> 01:26:21.980
than ever before, the
public is experiencing

01:26:21.980 --> 01:26:26.630
much greater difficulty
in accessing primary care,

01:26:26.630 --> 01:26:29.575
and we clearly need to do
something about we do it.

01:26:29.575 --> 01:26:32.240
- [Richard] We do.
- We've got a medium term

01:26:32.240 --> 01:26:35.990
approach in delivery of
Claire Fuller stocktake,

01:26:35.990 --> 01:26:39.500
which sets out a vision
for integrated primary

01:26:39.500 --> 01:26:41.180
and community services for the future.

01:26:41.180 --> 01:26:43.820
But that is very much
a medium term approach

01:26:43.820 --> 01:26:46.760
that some of the tasks required

01:26:46.760 --> 01:26:49.550
will take significant work and investment.

01:26:49.550 --> 01:26:53.017
But we do need to take some
really immediate actions

01:26:54.620 --> 01:26:57.620
to improve people's experience of access.

01:26:57.620 --> 01:26:59.960
I'm going to talk separately
about general practise,

01:26:59.960 --> 01:27:01.367
community pharmacy and dentists,

01:27:01.367 --> 01:27:04.970
but some of the messages
apply to all three.

01:27:04.970 --> 01:27:07.073
So, starting with general practise:

01:27:08.150 --> 01:27:12.560
we hear a lot of noise
about the experience

01:27:12.560 --> 01:27:13.850
the public are having,

01:27:13.850 --> 01:27:16.640
but most of that is about their experience

01:27:16.640 --> 01:27:18.920
in contacting services.

01:27:18.920 --> 01:27:21.260
So, people are finding it
difficult to get through

01:27:21.260 --> 01:27:22.913
to practises on the phone.

01:27:23.870 --> 01:27:26.600
Often appointments are
gone by the time they do,

01:27:26.600 --> 01:27:29.630
and we have this constant
cycle of redialing

01:27:29.630 --> 01:27:30.800
at eight o'clock every morning.

01:27:30.800 --> 01:27:32.570
We need to put a stop to that.

01:27:32.570 --> 01:27:36.560
So, we need to take steps to
address the issue of contact,

01:27:36.560 --> 01:27:40.700
and that's by better
cloud-based telephony systems

01:27:40.700 --> 01:27:42.530
that allow more lines in and out,

01:27:42.530 --> 01:27:44.660
that allow better signposting of patients,

01:27:44.660 --> 01:27:47.510
and importantly also give
us the data about people

01:27:47.510 --> 01:27:49.610
trying to call and when
they're trying to call,

01:27:49.610 --> 01:27:52.040
and letting us match demand and capacity.

01:27:52.040 --> 01:27:55.010
But we also need to
focus on digital routes

01:27:55.010 --> 01:27:57.827
to access primary care,
whether that's eConsults,

01:27:59.090 --> 01:28:03.890
use of the app or other digital
routes to view your records,

01:28:03.890 --> 01:28:07.970
view your test results, view
information about your care.

01:28:07.970 --> 01:28:09.830
Digital registration with practises,

01:28:09.830 --> 01:28:12.680
which will come in over
the next couple of months.

01:28:12.680 --> 01:28:14.510
We're piloting it now.

01:28:14.510 --> 01:28:15.800
From the 1st of November,

01:28:15.800 --> 01:28:17.960
everyone will have prospective access

01:28:17.960 --> 01:28:20.060
to the records digitally.

01:28:20.060 --> 01:28:21.860
All of that frees up capacity to deal

01:28:21.860 --> 01:28:23.030
with the people trying to get through

01:28:23.030 --> 01:28:24.383
on the phone every morning.

01:28:25.370 --> 01:28:27.980
But once we've started to make inroads

01:28:27.980 --> 01:28:29.540
into the difficulty with contact,

01:28:29.540 --> 01:28:31.970
we then need to increase
clinical capacity,

01:28:31.970 --> 01:28:34.193
and make that available for people,

01:28:35.240 --> 01:28:36.770
and there's three aspects to that.

01:28:36.770 --> 01:28:41.540
There's improving recruitment,
particularly around GPs.

01:28:41.540 --> 01:28:43.850
We've made massive improvements recently.

01:28:43.850 --> 01:28:46.160
We've had significant
increases in the numbers

01:28:46.160 --> 01:28:47.663
of GP trainees,

01:28:49.490 --> 01:28:52.970
and the issue we've got
though is that we need

01:28:52.970 --> 01:28:55.280
to retain those GPs once they qualify

01:28:55.280 --> 01:28:57.050
as general practitioners.

01:28:57.050 --> 01:29:00.800
About 48% of them currently
are international graduates.

01:29:00.800 --> 01:29:03.260
Their visas, their leave to remain,

01:29:03.260 --> 01:29:05.960
expires the day they
complete their GP training,

01:29:05.960 --> 01:29:08.390
and we are working with
government departments

01:29:08.390 --> 01:29:10.760
around addressing visa issues

01:29:10.760 --> 01:29:13.340
that make it much more difficult for us

01:29:13.340 --> 01:29:16.370
to plan to retain all of-

01:29:16.370 --> 01:29:17.960
- [Richard] You would expect me to comment

01:29:17.960 --> 01:29:19.970
that actually it should
be something that we,

01:29:19.970 --> 01:29:22.820
with a number of the
government departments,

01:29:22.820 --> 01:29:24.713
home office and the department,

01:29:25.880 --> 01:29:27.320
that we should be able to fix this.

01:29:27.320 --> 01:29:32.320
It's ridiculous that we train
almost 3,800, 4,000 GPs,

01:29:33.770 --> 01:29:35.810
who come to us internationally,

01:29:35.810 --> 01:29:37.400
and then on the day they essentially

01:29:37.400 --> 01:29:39.740
are finished their training,

01:29:39.740 --> 01:29:42.290
they're told that their
visas effectively are out

01:29:42.290 --> 01:29:44.720
until they find a sponsoring employer

01:29:44.720 --> 01:29:45.823
or however the system is.

01:29:47.660 --> 01:29:49.850
It's clearly not at all sensible,

01:29:49.850 --> 01:29:52.340
and one would've thought it
would be a very self-evident

01:29:52.340 --> 01:29:54.530
and straightforward exercise

01:29:54.530 --> 01:29:56.810
that we don't train these
GPs to find actually then

01:29:56.810 --> 01:29:59.210
that we force them to leave the country.

01:29:59.210 --> 01:30:00.740
Remarkable.

01:30:00.740 --> 01:30:01.940
- And we are working positively,

01:30:01.940 --> 01:30:03.830
particularly with the Department
of Health and Social Care

01:30:03.830 --> 01:30:05.600
to try and address this.

01:30:05.600 --> 01:30:09.920
But the other issue is
that while GPs in training

01:30:09.920 --> 01:30:11.570
are largely full time,

01:30:11.570 --> 01:30:15.383
so the average is their
0.98 whole time equivalent,

01:30:16.340 --> 01:30:20.390
that drops to 0.64 home
time equivalent on average

01:30:20.390 --> 01:30:22.850
once they become general practitioners,

01:30:22.850 --> 01:30:27.850
and we think we retain about
72% of that trainee headcount

01:30:28.940 --> 01:30:32.390
into substantive NHS
general practise posts.

01:30:32.390 --> 01:30:35.240
So, at every step along the
way, there is some attrition.

01:30:38.090 --> 01:30:41.420
Equally important or more
important, though, is retention.

01:30:41.420 --> 01:30:44.660
We are losing qualified
general practitioners

01:30:44.660 --> 01:30:46.790
faster than we can replace them.

01:30:46.790 --> 01:30:51.320
Since about 2010, the over 65 population

01:30:51.320 --> 01:30:54.350
has increased by more than 25%.

01:30:54.350 --> 01:30:57.590
That's the key driver of
workload in general practise.

01:30:57.590 --> 01:31:00.380
And in that time, the number
of fully qualified GPs

01:31:00.380 --> 01:31:04.100
working in the NHS has
reduced significantly.

01:31:04.100 --> 01:31:05.450
And that's the problem we've got.

01:31:05.450 --> 01:31:08.360
We've got a mismatch in
the demand from patients

01:31:08.360 --> 01:31:11.753
and the workforce and capacity
available to meet that.

01:31:12.710 --> 01:31:15.560
We need to address that workload.

01:31:15.560 --> 01:31:17.930
The workforce we have
are feeling overwhelmed,

01:31:17.930 --> 01:31:20.390
and therefore are less likely to stay

01:31:20.390 --> 01:31:22.580
or to stay working full time.

01:31:22.580 --> 01:31:24.830
And there is a pensions issue that, again,

01:31:24.830 --> 01:31:27.650
we're working with government departments

01:31:27.650 --> 01:31:29.630
around pensions flexibility,

01:31:29.630 --> 01:31:33.440
which stops a perverse
incentive to retire earlier

01:31:33.440 --> 01:31:35.180
amongst general practitioners.

01:31:35.180 --> 01:31:37.730
We also need to increase
our wider clinical capacity

01:31:37.730 --> 01:31:38.630
in our skill mix.

01:31:38.630 --> 01:31:41.180
We have the additional
roles reimbursement scheme,

01:31:41.180 --> 01:31:44.180
and we've recently
introduced more flexibility,

01:31:44.180 --> 01:31:47.330
removed some caps, added
some roles to that scheme,

01:31:47.330 --> 01:31:50.090
so that we can maximise
the benefit of being able

01:31:50.090 --> 01:31:53.030
to widen the skill mix
and use the allocation

01:31:53.030 --> 01:31:55.040
that we have to do that.

01:31:55.040 --> 01:31:56.390
But we also need to work better

01:31:56.390 --> 01:31:58.130
with broader primary care services,

01:31:58.130 --> 01:32:00.470
and particularly community pharmacy.

01:32:00.470 --> 01:32:03.140
So we've seen a really
significant increase

01:32:03.140 --> 01:32:05.510
over recent years,

01:32:05.510 --> 01:32:08.300
and particularly during
and since the pandemic

01:32:08.300 --> 01:32:10.790
in the move of community pharmacy

01:32:10.790 --> 01:32:14.510
from largely a dispenser of medicines

01:32:14.510 --> 01:32:16.460
to a clinical delivery service.

01:32:16.460 --> 01:32:20.750
So, community pharmacies
are offering thousands

01:32:20.750 --> 01:32:23.810
and thousands of consultations
for minor illness

01:32:23.810 --> 01:32:25.370
and injury each year.

01:32:25.370 --> 01:32:30.170
They are able to take part in
checking of blood pressures,

01:32:30.170 --> 01:32:32.810
in management of contraceptive services,

01:32:32.810 --> 01:32:37.730
in review of medication, in a
discharge medication service.

01:32:37.730 --> 01:32:41.960
All of this contributes
to offering some capacity

01:32:41.960 --> 01:32:44.900
that previously was taken
up in general practise.

01:32:44.900 --> 01:32:47.207
We need to continue to expand that.

01:32:47.207 --> 01:32:51.620
The community pharmacists
are a large source

01:32:51.620 --> 01:32:56.620
of not completely tapped clinical
resource in this country.

01:32:57.530 --> 01:33:00.980
But the sector is fragile.

01:33:00.980 --> 01:33:05.270
We made cost reductions to
the community pharmacy sector

01:33:05.270 --> 01:33:08.720
of about 8% during between 2016 and 2018,

01:33:09.813 --> 01:33:13.160
and have put 10% efficiency on the sector

01:33:13.160 --> 01:33:14.870
in the years since.

01:33:14.870 --> 01:33:17.622
It stands to reason that if
we want them to really step up

01:33:17.622 --> 01:33:22.622
to enable us to widen the part they take

01:33:22.880 --> 01:33:26.270
in delivering primary care services,

01:33:26.270 --> 01:33:28.400
then we do need to consider how we keep

01:33:28.400 --> 01:33:29.753
that sector sustainable.

01:33:32.390 --> 01:33:37.040
And then, dentistry:
equally, public satisfaction

01:33:37.040 --> 01:33:39.350
with access to dentistry has fallen.

01:33:39.350 --> 01:33:41.453
The reasons are slightly different.

01:33:43.310 --> 01:33:46.280
Routine dental services
dropped to practically zero

01:33:46.280 --> 01:33:48.800
in the early part of the pandemic.

01:33:48.800 --> 01:33:50.480
It's failed to recover since.

01:33:50.480 --> 01:33:53.810
So, even now, we're only
back up to about 80%

01:33:53.810 --> 01:33:58.340
of the NHS dental activity
that was being delivered

01:33:58.340 --> 01:33:59.900
pre-pandemic.

01:33:59.900 --> 01:34:02.510
That's largely because
we've seen a reduction

01:34:02.510 --> 01:34:06.140
in the relative amount
of NHS service delivery

01:34:06.140 --> 01:34:08.360
by dentists across the country.

01:34:08.360 --> 01:34:11.900
And so, we are actually
not seeing delivery

01:34:11.900 --> 01:34:15.290
of all of the dental
activity that we commission.

01:34:15.290 --> 01:34:19.460
We have had no reform
of the dental contract

01:34:19.460 --> 01:34:21.740
for the last 15 years until this summer,

01:34:21.740 --> 01:34:25.370
when we have made the first
steps in dental contract reform,

01:34:25.370 --> 01:34:29.000
which has been very positively
received by the profession,

01:34:29.000 --> 01:34:32.720
which is the first steps
in much bigger changes

01:34:32.720 --> 01:34:33.553
we need to make.

01:34:33.553 --> 01:34:37.790
But we have made provision
to encourage dentists

01:34:37.790 --> 01:34:40.190
to take on more of the complex patients,

01:34:40.190 --> 01:34:44.630
which they were not
particularly fairly remunerated.

01:34:44.630 --> 01:34:49.630
We're taking steps to increase the uptake,

01:34:50.430 --> 01:34:53.630
or the offer of dentistry
in parts of the country

01:34:53.630 --> 01:34:55.790
where there are dental deserts.

01:34:55.790 --> 01:34:58.430
And we've got more steps to
take in the immediate future

01:34:58.430 --> 01:35:02.990
around improving access to dentistry

01:35:02.990 --> 01:35:05.240
for people who don't have
a regular relationship

01:35:05.240 --> 01:35:06.470
with a dentist.

01:35:06.470 --> 01:35:08.780
So, within the data we've got on access,

01:35:08.780 --> 01:35:12.470
we can see it's much,
much easier to get access

01:35:12.470 --> 01:35:14.920
to dental services if
you already get access,

01:35:14.920 --> 01:35:17.180
if you already have a
relationship with a dentist.

01:35:17.180 --> 01:35:19.700
For people who don't have a dentist

01:35:19.700 --> 01:35:22.130
they have a relationship
with, it's very difficult.

01:35:22.130 --> 01:35:24.020
So, we're looking at how we encourage

01:35:24.020 --> 01:35:25.520
the offer of urgent dental care,

01:35:25.520 --> 01:35:27.380
particularly for that population.

01:35:27.380 --> 01:35:31.370
And again, we have a resource
we could tap into better

01:35:31.370 --> 01:35:34.070
in international dental graduates

01:35:34.070 --> 01:35:38.000
for whom there's a complex
and time-consuming,

01:35:38.000 --> 01:35:41.390
and for the dentist
themselves, expensive process

01:35:41.390 --> 01:35:46.390
in getting it onto the NHS
dental performance list,

01:35:46.460 --> 01:35:48.320
where it's very easy to
come into the country

01:35:48.320 --> 01:35:50.030
and work privately as a dentist.

01:35:50.030 --> 01:35:52.520
So, we're taking steps to address that.

01:35:52.520 --> 01:35:55.130
So, happy to answer any questions.

01:35:55.130 --> 01:35:57.320
- [Richard] You've given
us a very thorough,

01:35:57.320 --> 01:35:59.800
good and thorough walkthrough

01:35:59.800 --> 01:36:02.030
of what was actually a very good paper.

01:36:02.030 --> 01:36:04.250
I'll just pause and see
if any of my colleagues

01:36:04.250 --> 01:36:08.693
want to ask any questions
or make any comments.

01:36:09.534 --> 01:36:14.393
For me, I'd quite like to do
that deep dive into dentistry,

01:36:15.290 --> 01:36:18.020
really, to understand
what's going on in there,

01:36:18.020 --> 01:36:19.700
what the capacity is,

01:36:19.700 --> 01:36:23.270
what the dentist capacity
is in the country,

01:36:23.270 --> 01:36:25.280
that we can then think about.

01:36:25.280 --> 01:36:29.262
I was actually surprised when you tell us

01:36:29.262 --> 01:36:31.610
that we haven't actually done
that, looked at that contract

01:36:31.610 --> 01:36:34.151
in a material way for 15 years.

01:36:34.151 --> 01:36:39.151
So, it's well due a really
thoughtful approach.

01:36:39.890 --> 01:36:42.930
- [Wol] Sorry, is that
right? Apologies, Richard.

01:36:42.930 --> 01:36:45.080
We had a go looking at it,
which didn't do anything,

01:36:45.080 --> 01:36:46.223
about five years ago.

01:36:47.653 --> 01:36:50.060
We had a long conversation
about what could change,

01:36:50.060 --> 01:36:50.893
didn't we?

01:36:52.039 --> 01:36:53.180
- [Richard] I may have mis-described that.

01:36:53.180 --> 01:36:54.920
I don't think we've adjusted.

01:36:54.920 --> 01:36:56.420
- [Wol] We've not had
negotiations for 15 years,

01:36:56.420 --> 01:36:58.220
but we certainly had a good look.

01:36:58.220 --> 01:37:01.733
- [Julian] Think the department in place.

01:37:02.990 --> 01:37:04.880
- [Wol] We haven't-
- [Julian] piloted trial

01:37:04.880 --> 01:37:07.400
of changes to the dental contract,

01:37:07.400 --> 01:37:10.790
which were run for a period of time

01:37:10.790 --> 01:37:12.110
to see whether they worked.

01:37:12.110 --> 01:37:14.120
I don't think they've been taken forward.

01:37:14.120 --> 01:37:16.973
And so, now, we are implementing
nationally the steps

01:37:16.973 --> 01:37:20.600
that Amanda has taken.

01:37:20.600 --> 01:37:23.150
- In any event whether,
I've mis-described it,

01:37:23.150 --> 01:37:25.250
which is quite possible, I do think

01:37:25.250 --> 01:37:29.510
it behoves us to have
a really good look now.

01:37:29.510 --> 01:37:32.150
Community pharmacy, there's
some very good stats

01:37:32.150 --> 01:37:34.760
around blood pressure testing and so on.

01:37:34.760 --> 01:37:35.840
But at the same time, as you say,

01:37:35.840 --> 01:37:37.580
it's a fragile part of the infrastructure.

01:37:37.580 --> 01:37:39.560
We need to make sure that
we are properly supporting

01:37:39.560 --> 01:37:41.495
community pharmacists
to be able to provide

01:37:41.495 --> 01:37:44.093
this additional support.

01:37:45.290 --> 01:37:50.290
And then, as this Amanda
talks about a lot,

01:37:50.480 --> 01:37:55.480
as you also do, tangible
actions to take the pressure

01:37:55.790 --> 01:38:00.140
off primary care to enable
them to provide better access,

01:38:00.140 --> 01:38:01.310
but really tangible actions.

01:38:01.310 --> 01:38:03.950
You've listed several of
those in the paper to date,

01:38:03.950 --> 01:38:07.310
and just to keep on thinking
around how we can intervene,

01:38:07.310 --> 01:38:10.583
and in a supportive way will
be really, really helpful.

01:38:11.540 --> 01:38:14.900
Any other comments or
questions or shall we? David.

01:38:14.900 --> 01:38:17.750
- [David] I'm just struck
by the demand being driven

01:38:17.750 --> 01:38:20.990
demographically by people aged over 65

01:38:20.990 --> 01:38:22.730
and the challenge that that presents

01:38:22.730 --> 01:38:24.050
to the future workforce.

01:38:24.050 --> 01:38:25.970
So, I'd just reinforce the point.

01:38:25.970 --> 01:38:27.350
I think we have an opportunity, Richard,

01:38:27.350 --> 01:38:30.020
with a long term workforce plan,

01:38:30.020 --> 01:38:32.240
to really think through
what the skill mix is,

01:38:32.240 --> 01:38:33.650
that's required in primary care.

01:38:33.650 --> 01:38:35.180
And Amanda, you've spoken to that,

01:38:35.180 --> 01:38:37.790
so as well as the actions
that are described

01:38:37.790 --> 01:38:38.963
in the next steps,

01:38:41.900 --> 01:38:44.270
one of my takeaways from
what Amanda's just said

01:38:44.270 --> 01:38:46.490
is just the importance
of actually making sure,

01:38:46.490 --> 01:38:49.220
as we develop the long
term workforce plan,

01:38:49.220 --> 01:38:52.280
we're really clear what
the skill mix contribution

01:38:52.280 --> 01:38:53.960
to that is and then what the pipeline

01:38:53.960 --> 01:38:55.610
of those different roles are,

01:38:55.610 --> 01:38:57.530
and associated roles are going to be.

01:38:57.530 --> 01:38:58.733
Subject to the budget.

01:39:00.110 --> 01:39:00.943
- [Julian] Now, the
thing I was going to say,

01:39:00.943 --> 01:39:02.510
actually just picking up on that:

01:39:02.510 --> 01:39:05.780
Amanda's point about the reduction in GPs,

01:39:05.780 --> 01:39:08.900
which you really saw happen in about 2015,

01:39:08.900 --> 01:39:10.760
you can tie it to the tightening

01:39:10.760 --> 01:39:13.970
of the lifetime allowance threshold,

01:39:13.970 --> 01:39:16.250
and the time it takes
to start remedying that.

01:39:16.250 --> 01:39:18.200
Again, actually, the work
that the previous secretary

01:39:18.200 --> 01:39:19.670
of state, Jeremy Hunt, did to increase

01:39:19.670 --> 01:39:20.690
medical training places,

01:39:20.690 --> 01:39:25.690
which we are seeing now coming
through in the number of GPs,

01:39:26.570 --> 01:39:29.240
but it's just the time that it takes,

01:39:29.240 --> 01:39:31.940
and as we look at the medium term picture,

01:39:31.940 --> 01:39:34.660
how important it will be to find some way

01:39:34.660 --> 01:39:38.783
of protecting the investment
in medical places and training,

01:39:39.770 --> 01:39:42.440
because without it you
do just find yourself

01:39:42.440 --> 01:39:43.850
in quite big issues,

01:39:43.850 --> 01:39:46.231
which is what we're
currently experiencing.

01:39:46.231 --> 01:39:47.960
- [Richard] It was a chart
we've actually looked at

01:39:47.960 --> 01:39:48.793
as a board.

01:39:50.200 --> 01:39:51.560
You've got to be careful of start points.

01:39:51.560 --> 01:39:55.310
So we start the gradient
of growth in doctors,

01:39:55.310 --> 01:39:58.820
and if you started in 2010,
what we find is, don't we,

01:39:58.820 --> 01:40:00.710
that we could have virtually 20% increase

01:40:00.710 --> 01:40:02.003
in hospital consultants.

01:40:02.960 --> 01:40:06.323
In all GPs, we're under
10%, it looks like.

01:40:07.520 --> 01:40:11.150
No, no. Sorry I do apologise.
It's about 12% growth.

01:40:11.150 --> 01:40:13.880
But within that, fully qualified GPs,

01:40:13.880 --> 01:40:16.793
is actually flat to down,
in fact is mildly down.

01:40:17.930 --> 01:40:19.820
And that's a real challenge, actually.

01:40:19.820 --> 01:40:22.170
And to David's point,
there was that FT article

01:40:23.690 --> 01:40:24.910
and it quoted, didn't it,

01:40:24.910 --> 01:40:28.280
it showed us on a chart
that over 70s, not over 65s,

01:40:28.280 --> 01:40:33.280
the over 70s population has
grown by 30% since 2010,

01:40:35.030 --> 01:40:37.580
and the over 70s population
with comorbidities

01:40:37.580 --> 01:40:40.650
has grown by over 65% since 2010.

01:40:40.650 --> 01:40:42.830
So one can see the nature of the demand.

01:40:42.830 --> 01:40:46.220
A lot of that will fall upon primary care.

01:40:46.220 --> 01:40:49.610
So I think clearly, we've
got significant pressures

01:40:49.610 --> 01:40:50.630
we have to manage and Amanda,

01:40:50.630 --> 01:40:54.473
thank you for your
leadership in this space.

01:40:55.562 --> 01:40:56.613
So, can we go on then?

01:40:56.613 --> 01:40:58.220
I think Morag and Julian,

01:40:58.220 --> 01:41:02.900
we've got a briefer update on
the new hospitals programme.

01:41:02.900 --> 01:41:04.162
I don't know how you're going to do it.

01:41:04.162 --> 01:41:05.150
Why not hand it straight to Morag?

01:41:05.150 --> 01:41:07.070
- [Julian] Why not hand
it to Morag Stewart,

01:41:07.070 --> 01:41:08.736
who is the programme director

01:41:08.736 --> 01:41:10.053
for the new hospital programme,

01:41:10.053 --> 01:41:11.930
working very closely with the team

01:41:11.930 --> 01:41:13.910
in the department as well.

01:41:13.910 --> 01:41:15.530
But Morag, why don't I hand to you?

01:41:15.530 --> 01:41:16.910
- [Morag] Thank you.

01:41:16.910 --> 01:41:18.230
So yes, I'm Morag Stewart,

01:41:18.230 --> 01:41:21.230
chief programme officer for
the new hospital programme.

01:41:21.230 --> 01:41:22.070
Thank you for having us.

01:41:22.070 --> 01:41:25.190
We have prepared a paper,

01:41:25.190 --> 01:41:29.780
and a very, very brief update
is that we have 40 hospitals

01:41:29.780 --> 01:41:31.550
in our programme.

01:41:31.550 --> 01:41:36.470
Of those, 18 are funded under
the current spending review

01:41:36.470 --> 01:41:38.450
funding envelope.

01:41:38.450 --> 01:41:43.220
We've split the 40 into four cohorts.

01:41:43.220 --> 01:41:46.250
First cohort are well underway.

01:41:46.250 --> 01:41:48.950
There's eight in the first cohort.

01:41:48.950 --> 01:41:50.990
We have one that is open,

01:41:50.990 --> 01:41:54.470
and we have one that is going
through a phased opening

01:41:54.470 --> 01:41:56.120
at the moment in Royal Liverpool.

01:41:57.260 --> 01:42:00.860
We have 10 smaller schemes
that are going through now,

01:42:00.860 --> 01:42:03.530
and our objective as a
programme is to deliver those

01:42:03.530 --> 01:42:04.823
on time and on budget.

01:42:05.960 --> 01:42:09.950
And then, the next two
cohorts of of hospitals

01:42:09.950 --> 01:42:11.527
are really big hospitals,

01:42:11.527 --> 01:42:14.390
and we are transitioning
from a traditional way

01:42:14.390 --> 01:42:16.640
of designing and building a hospital

01:42:16.640 --> 01:42:20.360
into a much more centralised build design

01:42:20.360 --> 01:42:22.340
of healthcare,

01:42:22.340 --> 01:42:24.350
building on Amanda's point of delivering

01:42:24.350 --> 01:42:26.270
a really efficient system,

01:42:26.270 --> 01:42:28.970
and working with the integrated systems,

01:42:28.970 --> 01:42:32.750
integrated boards and
the region to deliver

01:42:32.750 --> 01:42:34.790
our part of that puzzle.

01:42:34.790 --> 01:42:39.770
So, we have four key
objectives as a programme.

01:42:39.770 --> 01:42:44.770
We are to deliver for
less time and less cost

01:42:44.900 --> 01:42:46.220
the hospitals in the future,

01:42:46.220 --> 01:42:48.590
and deliver in a very standardised way,

01:42:48.590 --> 01:42:50.543
using modern methods of construction.

01:42:51.860 --> 01:42:54.770
We have to ensure that
our hospitals deliver

01:42:54.770 --> 01:42:58.220
and integrate all of the
innovative national standards

01:42:58.220 --> 01:42:59.513
and are fully digital.

01:43:00.500 --> 01:43:04.040
We co-design with our local areas,

01:43:04.040 --> 01:43:05.420
and we build the foundations

01:43:05.420 --> 01:43:08.990
for an enduring national
capability for the future.

01:43:08.990 --> 01:43:11.210
So, that's a very short summary.

01:43:11.210 --> 01:43:12.043
Happy to take questions.

01:43:12.043 --> 01:43:13.640
- [Julian] Can I just add just two things?

01:43:13.640 --> 01:43:15.010
- [Richard] Of course.

01:43:15.010 --> 01:43:17.150
- [Julian] We are working towards

01:43:17.150 --> 01:43:20.990
the next big decision point
where we can really nail down

01:43:20.990 --> 01:43:25.250
total programme, total scope,
and as Morag alluded to,

01:43:25.250 --> 01:43:28.700
the funding currently covers about 18.

01:43:28.700 --> 01:43:31.460
We now need to nail down what
is the total programme budget

01:43:31.460 --> 01:43:32.360
for the whole lot.

01:43:32.360 --> 01:43:35.420
That is currently scheduled for December.

01:43:35.420 --> 01:43:38.960
And as part of that, we
also do need to consider

01:43:38.960 --> 01:43:42.380
how are we handling in
particular those hospitals?

01:43:42.380 --> 01:43:44.000
What we know as the rack hospitals,

01:43:44.000 --> 01:43:48.049
where we know there
are long enduring risk.

01:43:48.049 --> 01:43:52.130
There are about five of them
not currently in the programme,

01:43:52.130 --> 01:43:55.017
where with the government, we
at least need to work through,

01:43:55.017 --> 01:43:57.258
"Okay, how are we going to handle those

01:43:57.258 --> 01:43:59.658
in the run up to 2030?"

01:44:01.220 --> 01:44:02.120
- [Richard] Andrew?

01:44:02.120 --> 01:44:06.320
- These hospitals have got about 700 props

01:44:06.320 --> 01:44:08.990
holding the roof up.

01:44:08.990 --> 01:44:11.360
Just think about it:
you're being admitted,

01:44:11.360 --> 01:44:15.470
and your operating theatre,
what you see is all these props

01:44:15.470 --> 01:44:20.420
and the main bays have got a
similar kind of environment.

01:44:20.420 --> 01:44:22.220
And the thought of having that environment

01:44:22.220 --> 01:44:27.050
for nearly 10 years is
perplexing, to say the least.

01:44:27.050 --> 01:44:31.160
Anything we can do to
promote those projects,

01:44:31.160 --> 01:44:31.993
I'm all for it.

01:44:31.993 --> 01:44:35.720
Two, as you say Julian, are
in, but the others aren't.

01:44:35.720 --> 01:44:37.280
- [Richard] Can I ask?

01:44:37.280 --> 01:44:42.050
Do we know how many have
currently got this rack

01:44:42.050 --> 01:44:43.520
concrete problem.

01:44:43.520 --> 01:44:48.080
Are there more to come at us,
which were built similarly,

01:44:48.080 --> 01:44:49.348
but later?

01:44:49.348 --> 01:44:51.260
- [Morag] Not in England, no.
- [Richard] Not in England.

01:44:51.260 --> 01:44:53.840
- [Julian] So, there are a
larger number of hospitals

01:44:53.840 --> 01:44:58.010
who have this, but it tends
to be in a bit of the estate.

01:44:58.010 --> 01:44:59.830
We have a pretty well
established programme

01:44:59.830 --> 01:45:02.930
as to how, with the funding
we have and have been given

01:45:02.930 --> 01:45:06.800
by the government, where we have seen

01:45:06.800 --> 01:45:08.990
a reasonably significant overall increase

01:45:08.990 --> 01:45:11.810
in capital investment over
the last two or three years,

01:45:11.810 --> 01:45:15.710
a plan to eradicate rack planks,

01:45:15.710 --> 01:45:18.260
and except for, as I said,

01:45:18.260 --> 01:45:21.440
those five other major hospitals,

01:45:21.440 --> 01:45:22.910
not counting in the
new hospital programme.

01:45:22.910 --> 01:45:25.940
- [Richard] Just on the 18-
- Bring Jacqui in.

01:45:25.940 --> 01:45:26.773
- Sorry.

01:45:26.773 --> 01:45:31.773
Of the 18 that are funded,
are they fully funded,

01:45:32.030 --> 01:45:34.940
and do we understand what the inflation

01:45:34.940 --> 01:45:37.100
and growing build costs and pressures

01:45:37.100 --> 01:45:38.990
are going to do to that funding?

01:45:38.990 --> 01:45:40.940
- [Morag] So, we have
seven that are fully funded

01:45:40.940 --> 01:45:42.290
in cohort one.

01:45:42.290 --> 01:45:44.750
We have one which is
Moorfields Eye Hospital,

01:45:44.750 --> 01:45:48.140
which is just going through
its final business case now.

01:45:48.140 --> 01:45:48.987
That's cohort one.
- [Richard] Very good.

01:45:48.987 --> 01:45:51.980
- In cohort two, we have
the budgetary provision

01:45:51.980 --> 01:45:55.310
for cohort two, so that's
the next 10 schemes.

01:45:55.310 --> 01:45:56.390
But they have to go through

01:45:56.390 --> 01:45:58.550
an individual business case process

01:45:58.550 --> 01:46:01.103
to get their actual funding allocated.

01:46:01.940 --> 01:46:04.310
Then, for your other
part of your question,

01:46:04.310 --> 01:46:07.670
in terms of the inflationary
pressures on those hospitals,

01:46:07.670 --> 01:46:09.920
we've put a paper up now to treasury

01:46:09.920 --> 01:46:11.540
to explain the actual impact,

01:46:11.540 --> 01:46:14.060
so we've now actually
modelled out the full impact

01:46:14.060 --> 01:46:16.130
on those particular schemes,

01:46:16.130 --> 01:46:18.770
and treasury have asked
us to also model out

01:46:18.770 --> 01:46:21.920
for cohorts three and four when we go back

01:46:21.920 --> 01:46:23.900
for the main investment.

01:46:23.900 --> 01:46:26.390
- [Richard] Thank you.
And Jacqui, any comment?

01:46:26.390 --> 01:46:27.819
- [Jacqui] No, it's fine.

01:46:27.819 --> 01:46:29.630
It's just to add from
the rack perspective,

01:46:29.630 --> 01:46:31.637
we know where it all is,

01:46:31.637 --> 01:46:34.220
and it is actually in 14 hospitals,

01:46:34.220 --> 01:46:36.590
of which we have seven of those hospitals,

01:46:36.590 --> 01:46:38.030
which we've considered to be critical.

01:46:38.030 --> 01:46:40.790
So, the others are part
of our normal ongoing,

01:46:40.790 --> 01:46:42.620
we're dealing with it
as we're going through.

01:46:42.620 --> 01:46:44.300
I think it's just worth calling out

01:46:44.300 --> 01:46:48.470
that this is an ongoing
problem in the private sector,

01:46:48.470 --> 01:46:50.510
across his majesty's government buildings.

01:46:50.510 --> 01:46:54.650
We have the same issues in
schools and other areas,

01:46:54.650 --> 01:46:57.800
so we are actually working
across government as well,

01:46:57.800 --> 01:46:59.560
to look at the solutions on this.

01:46:59.560 --> 01:47:00.890
- [Richard] Very good, thank you.

01:47:00.890 --> 01:47:04.400
Any other comments, reflections
on new hospitals programme?

01:47:04.400 --> 01:47:07.730
Morag, thank you very much.
That was excellent. Thank you.

01:47:07.730 --> 01:47:09.230
Can we go on?

01:47:09.230 --> 01:47:13.280
I think now, Chris, it's to you and Bola,

01:47:13.280 --> 01:47:16.430
which is our annual report, isn't it?

01:47:16.430 --> 01:47:19.700
I think on NHS England's work
on healthcare inequalities.

01:47:19.700 --> 01:47:22.010
So, how do you want to do this?

01:47:22.010 --> 01:47:22.940
- [Chris] Thank you, Richard.

01:47:22.940 --> 01:47:25.610
Just to remind you that
the first principle

01:47:25.610 --> 01:47:28.520
in the NHS constitution
is that the NHS provides

01:47:28.520 --> 01:47:30.950
a comprehensive service available to all,

01:47:30.950 --> 01:47:32.630
and it states that we have a duty

01:47:32.630 --> 01:47:36.710
to pay particular attention to
groups or sections of society

01:47:36.710 --> 01:47:39.410
where improvements in
health and life expectancy

01:47:39.410 --> 01:47:42.530
are not keeping pace with
the rest of the population.

01:47:42.530 --> 01:47:44.720
That's why reducing health inequalities

01:47:44.720 --> 01:47:46.550
is one of the formal statutory aims

01:47:46.550 --> 01:47:48.530
for the new integrated care systems,

01:47:48.530 --> 01:47:50.660
and both NHS England and trusts

01:47:50.660 --> 01:47:53.900
have got newly enhanced
health inequalities duties

01:47:53.900 --> 01:47:55.880
in the 2022 Health and Care Act.

01:47:55.880 --> 01:47:58.040
And you'll remember
that the long term plan

01:47:58.040 --> 01:48:01.100
also commits us to further
strengthen actions we use

01:48:01.100 --> 01:48:02.904
in health inequalities.

01:48:02.904 --> 01:48:04.280
So, I think we all know that the cause

01:48:04.280 --> 01:48:06.470
of health inequalities
are many and varied,

01:48:06.470 --> 01:48:09.740
and the NHS can't solve
all of them on our own,

01:48:09.740 --> 01:48:13.100
and that's why we're focusing our activity

01:48:13.100 --> 01:48:14.990
on tackling the inequalities that exist

01:48:14.990 --> 01:48:16.790
in the delivery of healthcare,

01:48:16.790 --> 01:48:19.610
because it's that delivery
that's our direct responsibility

01:48:19.610 --> 01:48:21.080
and accountability.

01:48:21.080 --> 01:48:23.900
And if we want to support people into work

01:48:23.900 --> 01:48:27.380
and ensure they play their
full part in a growing economy,

01:48:27.380 --> 01:48:30.470
if we want to maximise NHS
productivity and efficiency,

01:48:30.470 --> 01:48:34.040
if we want to make services
sustainable in the long term,

01:48:34.040 --> 01:48:36.920
then we have to follow
what the data tells us

01:48:36.920 --> 01:48:39.350
on the gaps in healthcare
provision and outcomes,

01:48:39.350 --> 01:48:41.570
and then pursue those relentlessly.

01:48:41.570 --> 01:48:43.100
So, I'll give you just one example

01:48:43.100 --> 01:48:45.620
before I hand over to Bola,

01:48:45.620 --> 01:48:48.680
which was a recent report
from the British Red Cross

01:48:48.680 --> 01:48:52.430
suggested that less than
1% of our population

01:48:52.430 --> 01:48:54.620
account for a third of the demand

01:48:54.620 --> 01:48:57.020
in our urgent emergency care services,

01:48:57.020 --> 01:48:59.390
and that health inequalities
are a core driver

01:48:59.390 --> 01:49:00.500
of that demand.

01:49:00.500 --> 01:49:02.660
So, if you tackle those
health inequalities,

01:49:02.660 --> 01:49:04.730
we get a more sustainable service,

01:49:04.730 --> 01:49:07.137
a more efficient NHS,
and better care for all.

01:49:08.150 --> 01:49:10.760
That's why this agenda is so important.

01:49:10.760 --> 01:49:13.520
Bola, you are doing all
of the detailed work.

01:49:13.520 --> 01:49:15.080
- [Bola] Thank you so much, Chris,

01:49:15.080 --> 01:49:19.820
for that very important scene setting.

01:49:19.820 --> 01:49:21.350
I think in that context,

01:49:21.350 --> 01:49:24.050
then we needed to take
really focused action,

01:49:24.050 --> 01:49:26.360
given that it's impossible
to try and tackle it

01:49:26.360 --> 01:49:28.160
all at the same time.

01:49:28.160 --> 01:49:30.980
So, we came to the board about a year ago,

01:49:30.980 --> 01:49:34.460
and we put forward the
core 20 plus five approach,

01:49:34.460 --> 01:49:38.870
which focuses effort on the
20% most deprived communities,

01:49:38.870 --> 01:49:41.600
plus others experiencing

01:49:41.600 --> 01:49:44.720
those less than optimal outcome experience

01:49:44.720 --> 01:49:46.970
and access challenges.

01:49:46.970 --> 01:49:48.710
I just want to draw the board's attention

01:49:48.710 --> 01:49:53.000
to some specific examples of
what that means in practise.

01:49:53.000 --> 01:49:57.800
Mid and South Essex NHS
Foundation Trust for example:

01:49:57.800 --> 01:50:00.230
they've been doing a
piece of work on the NHS

01:50:00.230 --> 01:50:02.270
as an anchor institution,

01:50:02.270 --> 01:50:05.300
and providing employment
opportunities to people

01:50:05.300 --> 01:50:08.120
with learning disability and autism.

01:50:08.120 --> 01:50:10.850
And that means that people
are able to transition

01:50:10.850 --> 01:50:13.910
from school into meaningful employment

01:50:13.910 --> 01:50:17.870
as part of their health
inequalities project.

01:50:17.870 --> 01:50:22.280
In terms of productivity,
University Hospitals of Leicester,

01:50:22.280 --> 01:50:27.280
they realised they had 50% DNA
in their respiratory pathway,

01:50:28.280 --> 01:50:29.930
and when they analysed the data,

01:50:29.930 --> 01:50:32.750
they realised that
actually nearly all of that

01:50:32.750 --> 01:50:36.680
was accounted for by the
core 20 plus population.

01:50:36.680 --> 01:50:39.710
So, they worked with their
voluntary sector partners

01:50:39.710 --> 01:50:41.570
to support those communities,

01:50:41.570 --> 01:50:46.010
in terms of transportation
costs, parking costs,

01:50:46.010 --> 01:50:48.410
phoning people using the volunteer service

01:50:48.410 --> 01:50:50.780
that we were talking about earlier on.

01:50:50.780 --> 01:50:55.767
And in the last several months
they've gone from 4,000 DNAs

01:50:56.660 --> 01:51:00.920
in just the respiratory
services since '21, '22,

01:51:00.920 --> 01:51:04.340
and seen a 50% reduction

01:51:04.340 --> 01:51:07.940
just by taking that
health inequalities focus.

01:51:07.940 --> 01:51:09.770
Another example that I just want to bring

01:51:09.770 --> 01:51:12.860
to the board's attention
is Newcastle Hospitals

01:51:12.860 --> 01:51:14.150
NHS Foundation Trust.

01:51:14.150 --> 01:51:17.573
I was with them all day just
the day before yesterday,

01:51:18.500 --> 01:51:20.960
working in collaboration
with Health Works,

01:51:20.960 --> 01:51:23.660
which is one of their local charities

01:51:23.660 --> 01:51:26.420
supporting people on the waiting list.

01:51:26.420 --> 01:51:30.380
And actually, by providing
smoking cessation services,

01:51:30.380 --> 01:51:34.340
exercise classes, diabetes control,

01:51:34.340 --> 01:51:37.610
they have been able to
support people to exit

01:51:37.610 --> 01:51:39.110
the waiting list.

01:51:39.110 --> 01:51:41.180
And that is a demonstration of the NHS

01:51:41.180 --> 01:51:44.630
working in collaboration with
voluntary sector partners

01:51:44.630 --> 01:51:46.910
and our local authorities.

01:51:46.910 --> 01:51:49.880
I think, in terms of pressure
on urgent and emergency care,

01:51:49.880 --> 01:51:54.380
I draw the board's attention
to North Mids NHS Trust

01:51:54.380 --> 01:51:57.920
and the ABC parenting work they've done.

01:51:57.920 --> 01:52:01.190
They realised that actually a
lot of the A and E attendances

01:52:01.190 --> 01:52:04.580
were from people from
ethnic minority communities

01:52:04.580 --> 01:52:07.130
whose first language was not English,

01:52:07.130 --> 01:52:10.580
and struggled to access
the NHS in other ways

01:52:10.580 --> 01:52:13.880
other than turning up in
Accident and Emergency.

01:52:13.880 --> 01:52:18.410
And what they've seen with this
programme is a 25% reduction

01:52:18.410 --> 01:52:22.460
in Accident and Emergency
attendance from the families

01:52:22.460 --> 01:52:25.790
that have actually taken
part in that particular work.

01:52:25.790 --> 01:52:28.490
Finally, just on COVID vaccination,

01:52:28.490 --> 01:52:30.620
I think through the work
we've been doing with Steve

01:52:30.620 --> 01:52:34.790
and his team and indeed
going back to July, 2021,

01:52:34.790 --> 01:52:36.050
there is still work to do,

01:52:36.050 --> 01:52:40.700
but actually, by taking on
board the trusted voices

01:52:40.700 --> 01:52:44.120
of faith leaders, community leaders,

01:52:44.120 --> 01:52:47.780
making the vaccination more
accessible to communities,

01:52:47.780 --> 01:52:50.030
we have seen the gap close actually

01:52:50.030 --> 01:52:52.220
by five percentage points

01:52:52.220 --> 01:52:55.640
between the white British population

01:52:55.640 --> 01:52:58.280
and ethnic minority groups overall.

01:52:58.280 --> 01:53:01.400
And so, as the new
strategy is being developed

01:53:01.400 --> 01:53:03.620
around vaccinations and screening,

01:53:03.620 --> 01:53:05.420
we are working hand in glove to make sure

01:53:05.420 --> 01:53:08.537
that health inequalities
are central to that.

01:53:08.537 --> 01:53:10.520
And just in terms of next steps then,

01:53:10.520 --> 01:53:14.030
we've been working with
the chief nurses team

01:53:14.030 --> 01:53:17.360
in the children and young
people transformation team.

01:53:17.360 --> 01:53:20.990
So, we will be rolling
out core 20 plus five

01:53:20.990 --> 01:53:23.753
for children and young
people over the next year.

01:53:24.872 --> 01:53:26.030
Get my breath back.

01:53:26.030 --> 01:53:27.633
So, that's a really
important element of the work

01:53:27.633 --> 01:53:29.750
that we're going to be doing,

01:53:29.750 --> 01:53:33.170
as well as accelerating
focus on digital inclusion,

01:53:33.170 --> 01:53:35.300
which is incredibly important.

01:53:35.300 --> 01:53:39.200
It's one of the commitments
within the DHSC's

01:53:39.200 --> 01:53:41.180
digital and healthcare plan,

01:53:41.180 --> 01:53:44.240
and some of those actions are for the NHS,

01:53:44.240 --> 01:53:46.310
and we're working with the
transformation directory

01:53:46.310 --> 01:53:48.290
to drive those forward,

01:53:48.290 --> 01:53:50.600
and we've just set out
the other activities

01:53:50.600 --> 01:53:52.430
for the next steps in the paper.

01:53:52.430 --> 01:53:54.020
Thank you.

01:53:54.020 --> 01:53:56.933
- [Richard] Very good.
So comments for Bola?

01:53:58.730 --> 01:54:00.980
Let's go Wol, then David.

01:54:00.980 --> 01:54:03.110
- [Wol] I was looking, and
maybe you've covered this

01:54:03.110 --> 01:54:04.250
last year, apologies,

01:54:04.250 --> 01:54:08.840
but if I think of things like
multiple long term conditions,

01:54:08.840 --> 01:54:13.580
one of the big issues is
not so much the end state,

01:54:13.580 --> 01:54:14.510
but when you start with one,

01:54:14.510 --> 01:54:16.640
and how you go from one to many.

01:54:16.640 --> 01:54:20.540
Actually, in that journey
I think minorities

01:54:20.540 --> 01:54:23.630
tend to whole host of other reasons,

01:54:23.630 --> 01:54:27.740
be more hit, if you will, by that.

01:54:27.740 --> 01:54:29.540
And then the cost to
the system as a whole,

01:54:29.540 --> 01:54:34.220
once you have many long term
chronic conditions is huge,

01:54:34.220 --> 01:54:35.322
as we know.

01:54:35.322 --> 01:54:37.790
I couldn't quite understand
how you were monitoring that,

01:54:37.790 --> 01:54:38.900
how you'd been capturing that data,

01:54:38.900 --> 01:54:40.820
how we're making sure
that we're really focusing

01:54:40.820 --> 01:54:43.160
on the cost-cutting, issues like that.

01:54:43.160 --> 01:54:46.280
- Absolutely, and you're exactly right.

01:54:46.280 --> 01:54:49.910
The plus element of core 20 plus five

01:54:49.910 --> 01:54:54.260
actually has multi-morbidity
as one of the plus lenses

01:54:54.260 --> 01:54:55.760
that we're looking through.

01:54:55.760 --> 01:54:59.390
And actually, the whole core
20 plus five approach itself

01:54:59.390 --> 01:55:01.910
is a secondary prevention approach,

01:55:01.910 --> 01:55:04.070
very much looking at that multi-morbidity.

01:55:04.070 --> 01:55:07.760
So, we know that for some of
our South Asian communities,

01:55:07.760 --> 01:55:11.000
their diabetes starts 10 years earlier

01:55:11.000 --> 01:55:12.740
than the rest of the population.

01:55:12.740 --> 01:55:14.720
We've been talking to Ohid

01:55:14.720 --> 01:55:18.080
about the NHS health check programme

01:55:18.080 --> 01:55:20.780
and how we may need to flex that

01:55:20.780 --> 01:55:24.890
to reflect the differential,
both in incidence,

01:55:24.890 --> 01:55:27.980
prevalence and timing of onset of some

01:55:27.980 --> 01:55:30.020
of these long-term conditions

01:55:30.020 --> 01:55:31.370
in the way that you've just described.

01:55:31.370 --> 01:55:32.900
So, it's that multi-pronged approach,

01:55:32.900 --> 01:55:35.240
but it's very much part
of the core 20 plus five.

01:55:35.240 --> 01:55:36.544
- [Wol] Thank you.

01:55:36.544 --> 01:55:38.420
- [Richard] David.

01:55:38.420 --> 01:55:42.320
- [David] Thanks very much,
Richard, Bola and Chris.

01:55:42.320 --> 01:55:43.160
Thanks very much for this.

01:55:43.160 --> 01:55:47.240
I thought it was terrific
progress that you're identifying.

01:55:47.240 --> 01:55:48.073
And Richard,

01:55:48.073 --> 01:55:49.490
the question is more of a general one

01:55:49.490 --> 01:55:53.210
about how can we mainstream
what is set out here?

01:55:53.210 --> 01:55:56.330
We heard on the vaccination report

01:55:56.330 --> 01:55:59.270
about how important it
was to reach communities

01:55:59.270 --> 01:56:01.880
that we're not reaching,
and the onus is on us,

01:56:01.880 --> 01:56:03.440
not on the communities, to do that,

01:56:03.440 --> 01:56:07.400
which I think was a really
important contribution to make.

01:56:07.400 --> 01:56:10.340
And then, Bola, your
anecdote about A and E

01:56:10.340 --> 01:56:12.217
in North Middlesex and just
the difference you can make

01:56:12.217 --> 01:56:16.430
if you think about what
needs to happen to do that

01:56:16.430 --> 01:56:17.363
is tremendous.

01:56:18.770 --> 01:56:21.230
My question, really, is for
the wider executive team

01:56:21.230 --> 01:56:24.920
about how owned is this
strategy across all aspects?

01:56:24.920 --> 01:56:29.920
Because what I learned from one
of the conferences I went to

01:56:30.209 --> 01:56:31.970
that you spoke at, Bola;

01:56:31.970 --> 01:56:35.333
if you look at the elective
surgery waiting list,

01:56:35.333 --> 01:56:38.150
then the number of people
from deprived communities

01:56:38.150 --> 01:56:40.670
or Black, Asian and
minority ethnic backgrounds,

01:56:40.670 --> 01:56:45.670
that are represented on
those lists are high.

01:56:47.150 --> 01:56:50.630
And therefore, the issue
is not treating this work

01:56:50.630 --> 01:56:53.540
as being separate from the
other work we're doing,

01:56:53.540 --> 01:56:55.283
but how it's integrated.

01:56:57.380 --> 01:57:01.880
I've just looked to core 20 plus five,

01:57:01.880 --> 01:57:03.357
and interestingly, I
think your question, was,

01:57:03.357 --> 01:57:05.750
"What does CORE 20 plus five mean?"

01:57:05.750 --> 01:57:08.450
And I wonder whether there's times

01:57:08.450 --> 01:57:11.720
when we don't use the
acronym CORE 20 plus five.

01:57:11.720 --> 01:57:14.300
We actually say what it means,

01:57:14.300 --> 01:57:16.400
as a way of actually
mainstreaming it more,

01:57:16.400 --> 01:57:19.640
because it actually talks about maternity.

01:57:19.640 --> 01:57:20.900
Really interesting.

01:57:20.900 --> 01:57:23.990
Second out the box is mental ill health,

01:57:23.990 --> 01:57:27.020
really interesting given what
we've just discussed today.

01:57:27.020 --> 01:57:30.315
And it just strikes me as being actually,

01:57:30.315 --> 01:57:31.148
this should be mainstreamed,

01:57:31.148 --> 01:57:33.860
not a twice yearly report
about health inequalities

01:57:33.860 --> 01:57:35.060
coming to the board.

01:57:35.060 --> 01:57:36.560
- [Richard] Thank you.
- It's not a question.

01:57:36.560 --> 01:57:38.955
- [Richard] We should take
that away and think about that,

01:57:38.955 --> 01:57:41.229
how we deal with that, David.

01:57:41.229 --> 01:57:43.850
- [David] Actually, Bola
came to see me yesterday

01:57:43.850 --> 01:57:45.570
to discuss this very issue, actually,

01:57:45.570 --> 01:57:47.510
about how we mainstream it,

01:57:47.510 --> 01:57:49.370
and in some areas it's
easier to mainstream

01:57:49.370 --> 01:57:50.203
than others actually.

01:57:50.203 --> 01:57:52.490
So, the work that we've done
in the vaccine programme,

01:57:52.490 --> 01:57:55.250
we are immediately putting
that to work with flu, MMR,

01:57:55.250 --> 01:57:56.120
et cetera, et cetera,

01:57:56.120 --> 01:57:59.570
because we've really learned
much better how to do it.

01:57:59.570 --> 01:58:02.270
But you're right: the key
is absolutely mainstreaming.

01:58:03.710 --> 01:58:05.120
- [Richard] Very good.

01:58:05.120 --> 01:58:07.520
Bola, thank you very much, and
Chris, thank you very much.

